QB.push(
{id:1156,d:"D1",s:"Error Handling & Retries",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"A client requests `POST /v1/message` (singular) and separately tries to fetch a batch whose id no longer exists. Both calls return the same status code. Which status and `error.type` pair is it?",
o:["A. 400 with `error.type` of `invalid_request_error`","B. 404 with `error.type` of `not_found_error`","C. 410 with `error.type` of `gone_error`","D. 422 with `error.type` of `unprocessable_entity_error`"],a:1,
e:"**404 `not_found_error`** covers a resource that does not exist — a mistyped endpoint path, or a valid path with a resource id (batch, file) that is absent or already deleted. The fix is to check the endpoint path and any ids in the URL, not the JSON body. Because the URL is wrong rather than transiently unavailable, 404 is **deterministic**: retrying the identical request will produce the identical 404 forever. Route it to a code/config defect alert, never to a backoff loop.",
w:"A — 400 `invalid_request_error` signals a malformed or invalid body against a path that does exist; the docs note it also absorbs other unlisted 4xx conditions, but a missing resource has its own code. C — there is no documented 410 or `gone_error` on this API. D — 422 appears in some SDK exception tables as `UnprocessableEntityError`, but it is not the code returned for a missing resource."},

{id:1157,d:"D1",s:"Error Handling & Retries",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A newly issued API key works against the Messages API in the team's sandbox workspace, but every call from the production workspace returns:\n```\n{\"type\":\"error\",\"error\":{\"type\":\"permission_error\",\"message\":\"...\"},\"request_id\":\"req_01...\"}\n```\nThe key string is byte-identical in both environments. What is the correct diagnosis?",
o:["A. The key is expired or revoked, so it must be rotated","B. The request is missing the `anthropic-version` header in production","C. The key authenticated successfully but lacks permission for the resource, so organisation access and workspace settings must be checked","D. The production workspace has exhausted its token-per-minute allowance"],a:2,
e:"`permission_error` is the **403** body. The distinction from 401 is the whole point: **401 `authentication_error` means the credential itself is bad** (malformed, revoked, expired), while **403 `permission_error` means the credential is valid but not authorised for that resource**. Identical key bytes plus success elsewhere proves authentication works, so the defect is authorisation — organisation access settings or workspace scoping in the Console. Both are deterministic; neither should be retried. Fix the grant, then the call succeeds first time.",
w:"A — a revoked or expired key returns 401 `authentication_error`, and it would fail in the sandbox too. B — a missing `anthropic-version` header produces a 400 `invalid_request_error`, not `permission_error`. D — an exhausted allowance returns 429 `rate_limit_error`, and it would be intermittent rather than affecting every call."},

{id:1158,d:"D1",s:"Error Handling & Retries",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A document service base64-encodes a 30 MB scanned PDF, producing roughly 40 MB of JSON, and POSTs it to `/v1/messages`. The call fails immediately — fast enough that no model work could have happened. Which status and `error.type` is returned, and why immediately?",
o:["A. 400 `invalid_request_error`, because base64 padding is invalid at that size","B. 429 `rate_limit_error`, because the payload exceeds the input-tokens-per-minute allowance","C. 500 `api_error`, because the upload was truncated mid-flight","D. 413 `request_too_large`, because the byte size exceeds the Messages API request-size ceiling and the edge rejects it before it reaches the API servers"],a:3,
e:"**413 `request_too_large`** is a byte-size limit, enforced per endpoint (the Messages and Token Counting endpoints share one ceiling; the Batch and Files endpoints allow far more). On the direct Claude API the edge layer rejects an oversized body **before it reaches the API servers**, which is why failure is instant. Two consequences: it is deterministic, so retrying is pure waste; and `count_tokens` cannot predict it, because 413 is about bytes, not tokens. The fix is the **Files API** or splitting the document.",
w:"A — malformed base64 does produce a 400, but this payload is well-formed and simply too large. B — 429 is a rate limit over a time window, not a single-request size ceiling, and it would arrive with `retry-after`. C — 500 `api_error` is an internal Anthropic fault; a size violation is a client-side defect with its own code."},

{id:1159,d:"D1",s:"Error Handling & Retries",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"After a botched secret rotation, a worker pool starts receiving 401 `authentication_error`. The retry wrapper treats any non-2xx as transient and retries each call up to eight times with exponential backoff. What is the practical effect on the incident?",
o:["A. Time-to-detection lengthens and load amplifies eightfold, because a bad credential cannot self-heal through retrying","B. The pool recovers automatically once backoff exceeds the credential cache TTL","C. Requests eventually succeed because 401 responses are re-signed by the SDK on retry","D. The API returns 429 instead after the third attempt, which correctly triggers throttling"],a:0,
e:"A 401 is **deterministic**: nothing about the request changes between attempts, so every retry reproduces it. Retrying an authentication failure costs three things — **latency** (each user request now takes the full backoff ladder before failing), **load** (8× the request volume against an already-failing dependency), and, worst, **signal**. The error rate looks like a flapping dependency instead of a config defect. Transferable principle: **retry only what a later attempt could plausibly change** — transient connection faults, 429, and 5xx. Deterministic 4xx belongs on an alert, not in a backoff loop.",
w:"B — nothing in the 401 path is time-dependent; backoff cannot repair a wrong credential. C — SDKs re-send the same credential they were configured with; they do not re-derive or re-sign keys between retries. D — the API does not convert a 401 into a 429; the status reflects the actual failure condition each time."},

{id:1160,d:"D1",s:"Error Handling & Retries",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"Two incidents a week apart. In incident A, one team's error dashboard shows 429s while other teams in the same organisation are unaffected and the `anthropic-ratelimit-*` headers show that team's token allowance at zero. In incident B, 529s appear across every team and every workload simultaneously, with allowances showing plenty of headroom. What is the correct engineering response to each?",
o:["A. Both are capacity problems on Anthropic's side; open a support ticket for each and wait","B. Both should be handled by the same fixed-delay retry, since the root cause is queueing either way","C. A is the team's own allowance being consumed, so shape their demand; B is platform-wide overload, so back off, shed load, and degrade gracefully","D. A means the organisation must upgrade hardware; B means the API key must be re-issued"],a:2,
e:"**429 `rate_limit_error` is about you** — your organisation, workspace, or model-class allowance, or a tier spend cap. It is fixed by shaping your own demand: throttling, queueing, caching, spreading traffic, or requesting higher limits. **529 `overloaded_error` is about the platform** — high traffic across all users. No amount of limit-raising helps; the correct response is backoff with jitter, a circuit breaker, load shedding, and a degraded mode. Diagnosing which you have takes seconds if you record status code plus the rate-limit headers on every failure.",
w:"A — a 429 driven by your own allowance is yours to shape, and a ticket will not change your consumption pattern. B — the two need opposite responses, and a fixed delay is exactly the pattern that synchronises retries into waves. D — allowances are not hardware, and a 529 says nothing about your credential."},

{id:1161,d:"D1",s:"Error Handling & Retries",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A fan-out job runs 400 workers. Each worker uses textbook exponential backoff — 1 s, 2 s, 4 s, 8 s — with no randomisation. A brief capacity dip 429s most of them within the same 200 ms window. Over the next minute the dashboard shows four sharp spikes of 429s rather than a smooth recovery. What explains the spikes?",
o:["A. Exponential backoff is the wrong curve; linear backoff would smooth the recovery","B. All workers were throttled at nearly the same instant, so a deterministic schedule keeps them in lockstep and they collide again at every retry tier","C. The `retry-after` header resets the backoff counter on each 429","D. The backoff ceiling of 8 s is below the rate-limit replenishment window"],a:1,
e:"This is the **thundering herd**. Exponential backoff is correct in shape, but if every client computes the *same* delay from the *same* start time, the whole fleet moves as one block — 400 requests at t+1 s, 400 at t+3 s, and so on. Each wave re-saturates the allowance and re-throttles everyone, so the herd never disperses. The fix is **jitter**: randomise each delay so retries spread across the interval. Backoff controls total pressure over time; **jitter controls the shape of that pressure**, and you need both.",
w:"A — linear backoff synchronises just as badly and applies more total pressure; the curve is not the defect. C — `retry-after` tells you a minimum wait; it does not reset a client-side attempt counter, and here the header is being ignored entirely. D — the ceiling affects how long recovery takes, not why arrivals bunch into discrete spikes."},

{id:1162,d:"D1",s:"Error Handling & Retries",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"In a retry policy, what does adding jitter to an exponential backoff delay actually accomplish?",
o:["A. It shortens the average wait, improving tail latency under load","B. It guarantees each client retries at most once per rate-limit window","C. It makes the delay unpredictable to the server, preventing request fingerprinting","D. It de-correlates retry times across independent clients so their attempts spread out instead of arriving in synchronised waves"],a:3,
e:"Jitter is **de-correlation**, not speed. Backoff decides how much total pressure a failing dependency receives over time; jitter decides whether that pressure lands as a spike or a spread. Concretely, instead of sleeping exactly `base * 2^attempt`, you sleep a random value drawn from that interval — for example `random(0, min(cap, base * 2^attempt))`. Independent clients that failed at the same instant then retry at different instants. Without jitter, a fleet that was throttled together stays throttled together, wave after wave.",
w:"A — jitter changes variance, not the fundamental growth of the delay; individual waits may be shorter or longer. B — nothing about randomisation caps attempts per window; that is the job of a retry budget or maximum-attempts setting. C — retry timing is not a security control, and the server is not fingerprinting your delays."},

{id:1163,d:"D1",s:"Error Handling & Retries",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A finance lead sets a monthly spend limit on the production workspace in the Console, well below the organisation's tier cap. Mid-month, every request begins failing with a **400** whose message begins \"You have reached your specified workspace API usage limits\". The on-call engineer, seeing a 4xx, starts diffing the request body against staging. What has actually happened?",
o:["A. A spend limit that the organisation configured for itself is enforced as a 400 `invalid_request_error`, so the body is fine and the fix is a billing action","B. The workspace token-per-minute allowance is exhausted and will replenish within the minute","C. The API key was scoped to the wrong workspace and must be re-issued","D. The request exceeded the per-request byte ceiling and must be split"],a:0,
e:"This is the classic mis-triage. A **spend limit you set yourself** — organisation or workspace — is surfaced as **400 `invalid_request_error`**, not 402 or 429. The status code says \"4xx, your side\", which is technically true, but the fix is to raise or remove the limit in the Console, not to change the payload. Two lessons: **branch on `error.type` and read `error.message`, never on the status class alone**; and treat the message prefix as the tell here. Note the exception — limits on the Claude Code workspace can return a 429 with `retry-after` instead.",
w:"B — a per-minute allowance failure is 429 `rate_limit_error` and carries `retry-after`; this message names a spend limit. C — a workspace-scoping problem is a 403 `permission_error`, and it would fail from the first request rather than mid-month. D — a byte-size violation is 413 `request_too_large` and has nothing to do with cumulative spend."},

{id:1164,d:"D1",s:"Error Handling & Retries",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"On the 22nd of the month an integration starts receiving 429 `rate_limit_error` on every call. The SDK's automatic retries fire and fail; the team's own backoff ladder runs to 60 s and fails. Inspecting a raw response, they find **no `retry-after` header** and `error.details.error_code` set to `enforced_spend_limit_reached`. What is happening and what should the client do?",
o:["A. Priority Tier capacity was exhausted; switch to standard capacity headers to continue","B. The organisation has hit its usage tier's monthly spend cap, so retrying cannot succeed until access resumes or the tier is raised — the client must stop retrying and escalate","C. The organisation hit an acceleration limit from a sudden traffic ramp; keep backing off and it will clear within minutes","D. The `retry-after` header was stripped by a proxy; assume a 60 s delay and continue retrying"],a:1,
e:"A **usage-tier monthly spend cap** is reported as **429 `rate_limit_error`** — the same type as an ordinary rate limit — but it is not transient. Two documented tells separate them: the spend-cap 429 carries **no `retry-after` header**, and on the Messages API `error.details.error_code` is **`enforced_spend_limit_reached`**. Usage pauses until the start of the next month unless the limit is raised. Retrying — including the SDK's built-in retries — will keep failing. Branch on that `error_code`, stop the retry loop, and page a human for a billing decision.",
w:"A — Priority Tier exhaustion reports through `anthropic-priority-*` headers and does not carry this `error_code`. C — an acceleration limit does clear as traffic normalises and does carry `retry-after`; the absent header plus the `error_code` rules it out. D — assuming a delay and continuing is precisely the failure mode; the header is absent by design because no retry will succeed."},

{id:1165,d:"D1",s:"Error Handling & Retries",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A corporate card on the organisation's account expires. Overnight, a batch pipeline receives **402 `billing_error`** and its generic handler retries each of 12,000 items with exponential backoff for six hours before giving up. Nothing succeeds. What should the handler have done instead?",
o:["A. Fallen back to a cheaper model tier, since 402 indicates the requested model is not covered by the current plan","B. Retried with a longer ceiling, since billing state is refreshed asynchronously and eventually clears","C. Classified 402 as non-retryable, failed fast, and raised an operational alert for a human billing action","D. Re-authenticated with a fresh token, since 402 is a downstream symptom of credential drift"],a:2,
e:"**402 `billing_error`** means there is a problem with billing or payment information on the account. No client-side change — different model, longer wait, new token — can resolve it; someone must update payment details in the Console (or in AWS Marketplace on that platform). It therefore belongs in the **deterministic, non-retryable** bucket alongside 400, 401, 403, 404 and 413. The engineering value of correct classification is that a 402 becomes a page to the account owner within minutes rather than six hours of silent, billed-nothing churn.",
w:"A — model coverage and entitlement problems surface as 403 `permission_error` or a 400, not 402. B — billing state changes when a human updates payment details, not on a timer, so a longer ceiling only wastes more time. D — a credential problem is 401 `authentication_error`; 402 arrives on a perfectly valid key."},

{id:1166,d:"D1",s:"Error Handling & Retries",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"The Messages API enforces rate limits along which three dimensions per model class?",
o:["A. Concurrent connections, total tokens per minute, and requests per day","B. Requests per minute, total tokens per day, and concurrent streams","C. Requests per second, context-window occupancy, and cache-write volume","D. Requests per minute, input tokens per minute, and output tokens per minute"],a:3,
e:"The three limiters are **RPM (requests per minute)**, **ITPM (input tokens per minute)** and **OTPM (output tokens per minute)**, applied **separately per model class** — so traffic to different model classes draws from different buckets. Exceeding **any one** of the three yields a 429. This matters for diagnosis: a 429 with request capacity remaining but input-token capacity at zero calls for smaller or better-cached prompts, whereas one with output-token capacity at zero calls for shorter generations. One symptom, three very different fixes.",
w:"A — concurrent-connection and per-day request limits are not the documented Messages API rate-limit dimensions. B — the token limits are per minute and are split into input and output, not a single per-day total. C — cache-write volume and context occupancy are cost and capacity concerns, not rate-limit dimensions."},

{id:1167,d:"D1",s:"Error Handling & Retries",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A platform team builds a proactive throttle that reads only `anthropic-ratelimit-tokens-remaining` and pauses when it falls below a threshold. It works for months, then a workload that generates very long responses starts taking 429s despite the throttle reporting comfortable headroom. What is the flaw?",
o:["A. The `anthropic-ratelimit-tokens-*` headers report the most restrictive limit currently in effect, so a single aggregate reading can mask which specific dimension is nearly exhausted","B. Token headers are only populated on responses that already returned a 429, so the throttle reads stale values","C. The header is rounded to the nearest thousand, and rounding error alone accounts for the missed throttling","D. Streaming responses omit rate-limit headers entirely, so the throttle never sees output-heavy traffic"],a:0,
e:"The generic `anthropic-ratelimit-tokens-*` family shows the values for the **most restrictive limit currently in effect** — a useful summary, but a summary. A throttle that consumes only the aggregate cannot tell an input-token squeeze from an output-token squeeze, and can be comfortable on the reported limiter while a different one runs dry. The fix is to read the **dimension-specific** headers — `anthropic-ratelimit-requests-*`, `anthropic-ratelimit-input-tokens-*` and `anthropic-ratelimit-output-tokens-*` — and gate on the tightest of the three, each with its own `-limit`, `-remaining` and `-reset`.",
w:"B — rate-limit headers accompany successful responses too; reading them on success is the entire basis of proactive throttling. C — remaining token counts are rounded to the nearest thousand, but that is a small safety margin, not the cause of systematic misses. D — streamed responses carry the same rate-limit response headers as buffered ones."},

{id:1168,d:"D1",s:"Error Handling & Retries",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A summarisation service takes 429s whose headers show output-token capacity exhausted while request and input-token capacity sit idle. An engineer lowers `max_tokens` from 8192 to 1024 across the fleet and redeploys. Throttling is unchanged, and some summaries now truncate with `stop_reason: \"max_tokens\"`. Why did this fail?",
o:["A. `max_tokens` must be lowered on every model class simultaneously, and one class was missed","B. `max_tokens` does not factor into output-token rate-limit calculations, which count only tokens actually generated — so the change relieved no throttling and only truncated output","C. Lowering `max_tokens` raised the request count as clients re-called for continuations, cancelling out the saving","D. Output-token limits are evaluated against `max_tokens` at request admission, so the new value takes effect only after the limit window resets"],a:1,
e:"OTPM limits are **evaluated in real time as output tokens are produced, counting only the actual tokens generated**. `max_tokens` is a *ceiling*, not a reservation — it never factors into OTPM accounting, so there is **no rate-limit downside to a generous `max_tokens`** and no rate-limit upside to shrinking it. This is a widely held misconception worth unlearning: relieving OTPM pressure means generating genuinely less text (tighter prompting, lower reasoning effort, terser output formats), spreading traffic over time, or moving non-urgent work to the Batch API.",
w:"A — the parameter has no effect on OTPM accounting on any model class, so a complete rollout would change nothing either. C — the truncations are a real side effect, but the premise that `max_tokens` was throttling anything is the actual error. D — there is no admission-time reservation against `max_tokens`; accounting happens against tokens as they are emitted."},

{id:1169,d:"D1",s:"Error Handling & Retries",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"Which practice makes an error-handling layer most resilient to future Claude API changes?",
o:["A. Matching on substrings of `error.message`, since messages are more specific than type codes","B. Branching on the HTTP status class alone and ignoring the response body","C. Branching on `error.type` with an explicit default arm, and logging the full body plus `request_id` for anything unrecognised","D. Caching the first error type seen per endpoint and assuming subsequent failures are the same class"],a:2,
e:"`error.type` is the **stable, machine-readable discriminator**; `error.message` is human-facing prose that can be reworded at any time. Substring matching on messages is the single most common source of silent breakage. Equally important, the versioning policy states that the values inside the error object **may expand over time** — new `type` values will appear. So a `switch` on `error.type` needs a **default arm** that fails safe (treat as non-retryable, log, alert) rather than falling through to an unintended branch. Same discipline as handling an unknown `stop_reason`.",
w:"A — messages are not part of the versioning contract and can change without notice, breaking every substring match. B — status class alone conflates a configured spend limit with a malformed body (both 400) and a tier spend cap with an ordinary rate limit (both 429). D — errors on the same endpoint routinely differ between calls; caching a classification guarantees mis-handling."},

{id:1170,d:"D1",s:"Error Handling & Retries",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"A code review flags this handler:\n```\nswitch (err.error.type) {\n  case \"rate_limit_error\": return retryWithBackoff();\n  case \"overloaded_error\": return retryWithBackoff();\n  case \"api_error\":        return retryWithBackoff();\n  case \"invalid_request_error\": return fail(err);\n}\n```\nWhat is the most important defect?",
o:["A. `api_error` should not be retried, because 500s indicate a permanent server defect","B. The cases should be ordered by HTTP status code so the most severe is matched first","C. `overloaded_error` and `rate_limit_error` should share one branch to avoid duplication","D. There is no default arm, so any error type not listed — including types added in future — falls through unhandled"],a:3,
e:"The switch covers four types and silently drops everything else: `authentication_error`, `billing_error`, `permission_error`, `not_found_error`, `request_too_large`, and — critically — **types that do not exist yet**. The API's versioning policy explicitly permits the values inside the error object to **grow over time**, so an exhaustive-today switch is non-exhaustive tomorrow. Every enum-like field from this API (`error.type`, `stop_reason`, stream event types) needs a **default branch**. Fail closed: treat the unknown as non-retryable, log the body and `request_id`, and alert.",
w:"A — 500 `api_error` is an internal fault that the docs explicitly say to retry with exponential backoff, escalating with the request ID only if it persists. B — `switch` matches by value, not severity; ordering changes nothing about correctness here. C — collapsing the two branches is a cosmetic tidy-up, and arguably wrong since 429 and 529 warrant different backoff and alerting policies."},

{id:1171,d:"D1",s:"Error Handling & Retries",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"An intermittent 500 affects roughly one call in two thousand. The team's structured logs record timestamp, endpoint, status code and `error.message`. Support asks them to identify specific failed calls and they cannot. What must change, and where does the value come from on calls that succeed?",
o:["A. Log the `request_id` from the error body and the `request-id` response header, which is present on every response — success or failure — and is exposed by the SDKs","B. Log the `anthropic-organization-id` header, which uniquely identifies each call within the organisation","C. Enable request-body logging, since Anthropic reconstructs the call from a payload hash","D. Log the SSE `message_start` event id, which is the only per-call identifier available"],a:0,
e:"**Every API response carries a `request-id` header** (note the spelling — it is `request-id`, *not* `x-request-id`), and error bodies additionally expose the same value as a **top-level `request_id`** field alongside `type` and `error`. That is the identifier support needs. The SDKs surface it without raw-HTTP plumbing: Python and TypeScript expose `_request_id` on top-level response objects, while other SDKs expose it through raw-response accessors or middleware. Log it on **success as well as failure** — correlating a bad output with a healthy-looking 200 needs the id just as much.",
w:"B — `anthropic-organization-id` identifies the organisation, which is constant across all calls and cannot single one out. C — Anthropic identifies calls by request ID; logging bodies raises data-handling risk without solving the problem. D — an event id inside a stream does not exist as a support identifier, and it would cover only streamed calls."},

{id:1172,d:"D1",s:"Error Handling & Retries",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"An agent tool calls an internal payments service, then reports the result to Claude. A network timeout occurs 25 seconds into the payment call. The agent framework retries the whole model turn, Claude re-issues the same `tool_use`, and the customer is debited twice. Where must the idempotency control live, and why can it not live in the retry wrapper?",
o:["A. In the retry wrapper, keyed on a hash of the Claude request body, since identical model input implies an identical side effect","B. At the side-effecting layer — the payments call must carry a stable idempotency key per logical operation — because a timeout is ambiguous about whether the effect already happened","C. In the system prompt, instructing Claude never to repeat a tool call it has already issued","D. In the model layer, by setting the retry count to zero so no side-effecting turn is ever retried"],a:1,
e:"A timeout is **ambiguous**: the request may have been fully processed, partially processed, or never received. No client can distinguish these from the outside, so safety must come from the **receiver being idempotent**. That means a stable key derived from the *logical operation* (invoice id, order id) — not from the attempt, not from the request bytes — carried into the payments call, with the payments service deduplicating on it. Transferable principle: **retry safety is a property of the side-effecting boundary, not of the retry loop**. Put the key at the innermost mutation and every layer above becomes safe to retry.",
w:"A — hashing the model request keys on the wrong thing: two legitimately distinct payments can share input, and one payment can be retried with a mutated history. C — the model is not a security or correctness boundary; prompt instructions are advisory and cannot enforce exactly-once semantics. D — disabling retries trades duplicate charges for dropped work and still leaves the ambiguous timeout unresolved."},

{id:1173,d:"D1",s:"Error Handling & Retries",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"During a platform-wide incident, 529s run at 80% for eleven minutes. A service with a 6-attempt backoff ladder keeps every user request alive through the full ladder, exhausting its thread pool and taking down an unrelated endpoint in the same process. Which pattern would have contained the blast radius?",
o:["A. Raising the retry ceiling so fewer requests reach the final attempt","B. Increasing the thread pool so all in-flight retries fit","C. A circuit breaker that trips after a failure-rate threshold and fails calls immediately while open, with periodic half-open probes to test recovery","D. Switching from exponential to linear backoff to reduce the time each request is held"],a:2,
e:"Retries help when failures are **rare and independent**. When a dependency is broadly down, per-request retries multiply load on it and consume *your* resources — threads, connections, memory — until unrelated functionality starves. A **circuit breaker** watches the failure rate and, once it trips, **fails fast without calling the dependency**, releasing those resources instantly. Periodic **half-open** probes let a single request test recovery before the breaker closes. Retries handle transient blips; the breaker handles sustained outages. Production integrations need both, plus a bounded total retry budget.",
w:"A — a higher ceiling holds each request longer and consumes more of the pool, accelerating the collapse. B — a bigger pool raises the ceiling on a bounded resource but does not stop unbounded retry demand from reaching it. D — linear backoff shortens some waits but sends more attempts sooner, adding pressure to a dependency that is already saturated."},

{id:1174,d:"D1",s:"Error Handling & Retries",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A migration script iterates 90,000 records and fires a Claude call per record using unbounded `Promise.all`. It immediately takes a wall of 429s, and the retry logic makes throughput worse. Beyond adding jitter, what is the structural fix?",
o:["A. Split the work across several API keys in the organisation so each gets its own allowance","B. Drop `max_tokens` so each request consumes less of the rate-limit budget","C. Retry only the failures at the end of the run, treating the first pass as best-effort","D. Feed the work through a bounded queue with a concurrency ceiling calibrated to the observed rate limits, so the client paces itself rather than discovering limits by failing"],a:3,
e:"Unbounded fan-out converts a throughput problem into a failure problem: the client offers far more load than the allowance admits, and everything above the line becomes 429s plus retries — extra load carrying zero useful work. A **bounded work queue with a concurrency ceiling** makes the client the flow-control point. Size the ceiling from the `anthropic-ratelimit-*` headers and adapt it downward on 429. Transferable principle: **rate limits are a design input, not an exception to catch**. For genuinely large offline volumes, the Batch API is the purpose-built answer.",
w:"A — rate limits are enforced at the organisation level, so additional keys draw from the same buckets and change nothing. B — `max_tokens` does not factor into output-token rate-limit calculations, so lowering it relieves no throttling. C — deferring retries still submits 90,000 concurrent requests on the first pass, which is the actual defect."},

{id:1175,d:"D1",s:"Error Handling & Retries",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A reliability dashboard shows two panels climbing together over a deploy window: 4xx error rate up sharply, and 429/529 rate flat at baseline. Which conclusion should drive the on-call response?",
o:["A. The rise is almost certainly a client-side defect introduced by the deploy — a malformed body, bad credential, or wrong resource — so roll back or fix the code rather than tuning backoff","B. The organisation is approaching its allowance and should request a rate-limit increase","C. Anthropic is degraded; open a support ticket and wait for recovery","D. Backoff parameters are mistuned and should be widened until the 4xx rate falls"],a:0,
e:"The two panels answer different questions and deserve different runbooks. **Sustained 4xx (400/401/403/404/413) means your requests are wrong** — a schema change, a rotated secret, a deprecated parameter, a bad resource id. It is a code or config defect, it will not self-heal, and it should page the owning team. **Sustained 429/529 means capacity** — yours or the platform's — and should drive throttling, queueing, breakers and degraded modes. Keeping them as separate signals is what makes triage fast; a single blended \"error rate\" hides the distinction.",
w:"B — an allowance problem would show in the 429 panel, which is flat, and in the rate-limit headers. C — platform degradation appears as 529 `overloaded_error`, also flat here. D — no backoff setting can make an invalid request valid; widening it only delays the failure."},

{id:1176,d:"D1",s:"Error Handling & Retries",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A content pipeline's \"failure rate\" metric counts every unusable result. Investigation shows three distinct populations behind it: HTTP 400s from one caller, HTTP 529s during peak hours, and HTTP **200** responses whose `stop_reason` is `refusal`. What is wrong with the single metric?",
o:["A. Nothing — all three prevent a usable result, so one metric correctly captures business impact","B. It conflates integration faults, capacity events, and model-content outcomes, which have unrelated owners and remedies; the refusals are not transport failures at all","C. The 529s should be excluded because they are Anthropic's responsibility rather than the team's","D. The 200 responses should be reclassified as 400s, since a refusal means the request was invalid"],a:1,
e:"Three different systems are failing and only one metric is watching. **400 is an integration fault** — deterministic, owned by the caller, fixed in code. **529 is a capacity event** — transient, fixed with backoff, breakers and degraded modes. A **200 with `stop_reason: \"refusal\"`** is a successful, billed API call whose *content* was declined; it is owned by prompt and policy design, and `stop_details` (populated only for refusals) carries a `category` to analyse. Blending them means every remedy looks partially effective and none is decisive. Detect refusals via `stop_reason`, never by string-matching text.",
w:"A — business impact is one legitimate view, but a single number cannot route a page to the right owner or validate a fix. C — 529 rate is a first-class operational signal precisely because it drives your backoff and degradation behaviour. D — the request was valid and the call succeeded; relabelling a 200 as a 400 would send engineers hunting a non-existent schema bug."},

{id:1177,d:"D1",s:"Error Handling & Retries",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"Which statement best describes the retry behaviour built into the official Anthropic SDKs?",
o:["A. No retries are performed; all retry logic must be supplied by the caller","B. Every non-2xx response is retried up to five times with a fixed one-second delay","C. Transient failures — connection errors, request timeouts, 429 and 5xx — are retried twice by default with a short exponential backoff, honouring `retry-after` when present, and the count is configurable","D. Retries are attempted indefinitely until the configured request timeout elapses"],a:2,
e:"The SDKs ship a sane default so most callers need no retry code: **connection errors, 408 request timeouts, 409 conflicts, 429 rate limits and ≥500 server errors are retried twice**, with short exponential backoff, **honouring the `retry-after` header** when the response supplies one. Deterministic failures such as 400, 401, 403 and 404 are **not** retried. A **max-retries option** on the client tunes or disables this (`max_retries=0`), and it can be overridden per request. Knowing the default matters: adding your own loop on top **multiplies** attempts.",
w:"A — the SDKs retry by default; assuming otherwise leads teams to build a second, redundant layer. B — a fixed delay across all non-2xx would retry deterministic 4xx and synchronise the fleet, which is the opposite of the documented behaviour. D — retries are bounded by an attempt count, not by burning the whole request timeout."},

{id:1178,d:"D1",s:"Error Handling & Retries",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A team wraps the SDK client in a decorator that retries any exception up to five times. Under a brief 429 burst, their tracing shows a single logical call producing fifteen HTTP requests and a tail latency of well over a minute. What is the cause and the cleanest fix?",
o:["A. The tracing double-counts SDK-internal requests; only five HTTP calls actually occurred","B. Their five attempts each trigger the SDK's own default retries, multiplying the total; pick one layer to own retries — set the SDK's max-retries option and let it honour `retry-after`, or disable it and own the policy fully","C. The SDK escalates to a longer ladder when it detects an external retry wrapper, which must be turned off with a header","D. Five attempts is simply too many for a 429; reducing it to two resolves the multiplication"],a:1,
e:"Retry layers **compose multiplicatively**. Five outer attempts × three SDK attempts (initial plus two default retries) is up to fifteen requests, each carrying its own backoff — so the client hammers a throttled dependency while the user waits through nested ladders. The rule is **exactly one owner of retry policy per call path**. Either configure the SDK's max-retries option and let it do the work (it already honours `retry-after`), or set `max_retries=0` and implement backoff, jitter, budget and breaker yourself. Whichever you choose, assert it in a test so a future upgrade cannot silently re-enable the other.",
w:"A — the requests are real; each layer issues genuine HTTP calls against the API. C — there is no such detection or opt-out header; the SDK has no knowledge of your wrapper. D — fewer outer attempts reduces the product but leaves two independent policies fighting, so `retry-after` and backoff remain uncoordinated."},

{id:1179,d:"D1",s:"Error Handling & Retries",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"An endpoint returns 400 `invalid_request_error` only in one region's deployment. Same container image, same model, same code path; the message names a field the developers do not recognise. Which technique isolates the cause fastest?",
o:["A. Capture the fully serialised JSON body and headers actually sent from each environment at the client boundary, then diff them byte for byte","B. Re-run the failing prompt through `/v1/messages/count_tokens` in both regions and compare `input_tokens`","C. Increase the SDK's max-retries option in the failing region to see whether the 400 is intermittent","D. Compare `usage` objects from the last successful response in each region"],a:0,
e:"A 400 is **deterministic and specific**: the same bytes always produce the same verdict, so if one environment fails and another succeeds, **the bytes differ**. The fastest path is to log the request as the transport actually serialises it — body plus headers — and diff. Environment-specific culprits are mundane and invisible in source: config-injected extra parameters, a stale `anthropic-version`, a proxy adding or rewriting headers, an interceptor merging defaults, or a deprecated field surviving in one region's config (`temperature`, `top_p`, `thinking.type: \"enabled\"` and assistant prefill all now return 400 on current models). SDK debug logging or a raw-response accessor gives you this without guesswork.",
w:"B — `count_tokens` reports only `input_tokens` and validates nothing about the parameters causing the rejection. C — retrying a deterministic 400 cannot change the outcome and destroys the signal by inflating the error count. D — `usage` describes a call that already succeeded and says nothing about the rejected payload."},

{id:1180,d:"D1",s:"Error Handling & Retries",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"To \"fail fast\", a team sets the SDK client timeout to 8 seconds globally. Long report generations begin failing, and their metrics show request volume to the API roughly tripling while the user-visible error rate rises. What is going on?",
o:["A. The 8-second timeout is interpreted as a connect timeout only, so read stalls are unaffected","B. Requests exceeding the timeout are terminated client-side and then retried by default, so each doomed generation is attempted several times while the server keeps doing the discarded work","C. Timeouts are reported as 429s, which the SDK counts against the rate-limit allowance","D. The SDK ignores timeouts below 10 seconds and substitutes its default"],a:1,
e:"The SDK default request timeout is generous (about **10 minutes**) precisely because long generations are legitimate. Clamping it below real latency does not make anything faster — the client abandons the connection, raises a timeout error, and **timeouts are retried by default**, so one logical call becomes several full-cost server-side generations that nobody reads. Set the timeout above the p99 of the work you actually issue, per call path rather than globally, and for genuinely long jobs **stream** or use the **Batch API**: streaming keeps the connection productive and avoids idle-connection drops on intermediate networks.",
w:"A — the timeout option governs the overall request; granular connect, read and write control is available but is not what a single scalar sets. C — a client-side timeout is not a server rate-limit response and is not accounted against your allowance. D — the SDK honours the timeout you configure; it does not silently substitute its default."},

{id:1181,d:"D1",s:"Error Handling & Retries",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A report generator fans a single user request out into 200 independent Claude calls, one per section, and joins the results. On one run, 193 succeed and 7 fail with 429. The current code catches the first exception and aborts the whole job, discarding 193 completed calls. What is the correct partial-failure design?",
o:["A. Abort on first failure but cache nothing, so the retry is a clean run and results stay consistent","B. Treat any failure in the fan-out as fatal and surface a single error, since a partially complete report is misleading","C. Collect per-item outcomes, retry only the failed items with jittered backoff against a bounded attempt budget, and after the budget mark those sections explicitly incomplete","D. Reduce the fan-out to a single sequential loop so a failure only ever affects one item"],a:2,
e:"In fan-out, failure is **per item**, so recovery must be per item. Settle every branch independently, keep the successes, and re-drive only the failures — 7 retries instead of 200 means less load on an already-throttled dependency and far lower cost. Two disciplines complete the pattern: a **bounded attempt budget** so a persistent failure cannot stall the job forever, and **explicit degradation** — a section rendered as \"unavailable\" is honest, whereas a silently missing section is a correctness bug. In JavaScript this is `allSettled` semantics rather than `all` semantics.",
w:"A — discarding 193 successful, billed calls multiplies both cost and latency on every retry of the job. B — a clearly-labelled partial result is more useful than nothing; the misleading case is unlabelled partial output, which explicit marking solves. D — sequential execution turns a 200-way parallel job into a serial one and still leaves every failed item unhandled."},

{id:1182,d:"D1",s:"Error Handling & Retries",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"During heavy platform load, a streaming request has already returned HTTP 200 and delivered several `content_block_delta` events. How does the API communicate that it can no longer continue?",
o:["A. It silently closes the connection with no further data, so clients must infer failure from a missing `message_stop`","B. It retroactively changes the HTTP status to 529 on the same connection","C. It emits a `message_delta` with `stop_reason: \"error\"` followed by `message_stop`","D. It emits an `error` event whose data carries the standard error object — for example `{\"type\":\"error\",\"error\":{\"type\":\"overloaded_error\",...}}`"],a:3,
e:"Once the response is a 200, standard HTTP error handling no longer applies. The stream carries a dedicated **`error` event** whose `data` is the same error envelope you would see in a non-streaming body — so an `overloaded_error` that would have been an **HTTP 529** in a buffered call arrives mid-stream instead. Practical consequence: a streaming client cannot treat \"status 200\" as \"call succeeded\". It must handle `error` events explicitly, and — per the versioning policy — handle **unknown event types** gracefully too.",
w:"A — a defined `error` event is emitted; relying on inference would leave every mid-stream failure indistinguishable from a network drop. B — HTTP status is sent in the response head and cannot be revised once the body has begun. C — `stop_reason` is not an error channel and has no `error` value; the documented values are a separate, extensible enum."},

{id:1183,d:"D1",s:"Error Handling & Retries",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A chat UI streams responses. Its error handling lives entirely in an HTTP status check: `if (res.status !== 200) handleError()`. Users occasionally report replies that stop mid-sentence with no error shown and no retry offered. Monitoring records a 100% success rate. What is the defect?",
o:["A. Errors that occur after the 200 has been sent arrive as `error` events inside the stream, so a status-only check classifies every mid-stream failure as a success","B. The client is not reading `message_stop`, so the UI never learns the response ended","C. The proxy is buffering the SSE stream and truncating it at a fixed size","D. `stop_reason` arrives in `message_stop` and the client is reading the wrong event"],a:0,
e:"The status line commits before the first byte of the body, so **a 200 only means the stream started**. Mid-stream failures — most commonly `overloaded_error` during high load — arrive as **`error` events** in the event stream. A status-only check both hides the failure from users and poisons monitoring: the success metric reads 100% while replies truncate. A correct streaming client treats the terminal state as a function of the events: success requires the expected terminal events, any `error` event is a failure worth surfacing and retrying, and unknown event types are ignored gracefully.",
w:"B — not reading `message_stop` would break end-of-response handling for every reply, not occasionally, and would not explain the false success metric. C — proxy buffering is a real hazard for long streams but would not be reported as a clean 200 with an unhandled in-band error. D — the final `stop_reason` and cumulative output usage arrive in `message_delta`, and neither is how a mid-stream error is reported."},

{id:1184,d:"D1",s:"Error Handling & Retries",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A streaming client receives an `error` event carrying `overloaded_error` after roughly 400 tokens of text have already been displayed. What is the correct recovery, and what should the user see?",
o:["A. Send a follow-up request asking Claude to continue from the last displayed token, since the API resumes an interrupted generation from its cursor","B. Retry the request from the beginning with jittered backoff, discarding or clearly replacing the partial text, because there is no resume mechanism for an interrupted stream","C. Keep the partial text and mark the turn complete, since a 200 stream that produced text is a successful call","D. Immediately retry on the same connection, since the SSE channel remains open after an `error` event"],a:1,
e:"There is **no resume-from-offset** primitive for an interrupted generation. The API is **stateless**: recovery means re-issuing the request — same `messages`, same parameters — with jittered exponential backoff, and this counts as a fresh, separately billed call. The UI decision matters as much as the retry: partial text must be visibly replaced or discarded, never silently left as if complete, or downstream consumers and users treat a truncated answer as final. If cost from re-generation is a concern, **prompt caching** on the stable prefix reduces the input cost of the retry.",
w:"A — asking the model to \"continue\" is a new request whose output cannot be guaranteed to join cleanly, and no cursor-based resume exists. C — a stream terminated by an `error` event did not complete; treating it as success is exactly the bug that hides mid-stream failures from monitoring. D — the stream has ended; a retry is a new HTTP request, and reusing the terminated event channel is not possible."},

{id:1185,d:"D1",s:"Error Handling & Retries",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"Which response header carries the per-call identifier that Anthropic support asks for?",
o:["A. `x-request-id`","B. `anthropic-trace-id`","C. `request-id`","D. `x-anthropic-correlation-id`"],a:2,
e:"The header is **`request-id`** — no `x-` prefix — and it is present on **every** response, containing a value such as `req_018EeWyXxfu5pfWkrYcMdjWG`. The same value appears as the top-level **`request_id`** field in error bodies. This trips people up because `x-request-id` is the convention at many other providers, so log-grepping for the wrong spelling returns nothing and teams conclude the identifier is missing. SDK shortcuts exist: `_request_id` on Python and TypeScript response objects, raw-response accessors elsewhere. On Claude Platform on AWS you also get `x-amzn-requestid` for CloudTrail lookups.",
w:"A — `x-request-id` is the common convention elsewhere but is not the header this API sends. B — there is no `anthropic-trace-id` header; documented `anthropic-*` response headers cover rate limits, organisation and workspace. D — no such correlation header is defined; correlation is done by joining `request-id` to your own trace id in logs."},

{id:1186,d:"D1",s:"Error Handling & Retries",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A logging middleware written against a different provider records `res.headers['x-request-id']`. Every Claude API log line shows `requestId: null`, including on failures, and support tickets stall. What is the minimal correct change?",
o:["A. Enable verbose SDK logging permanently, since headers are only exposed in debug mode","B. Parse the identifier out of `error.message`, which embeds it on failures","C. Generate a client-side UUID per call and send it as `x-request-id` so both sides share an identifier","D. Log the `request-id` header (and the top-level `request_id` in error bodies), reading it via the SDK's raw-response accessor or `_request_id` property"],a:3,
e:"A one-word fix: the header is **`request-id`**, and the SDKs expose it without raw-HTTP work — `_request_id` on Python and TypeScript response objects, raw-response accessors on the C#, Go, Java and PHP SDKs, middleware on Ruby. Error bodies carry the same value as top-level **`request_id`**. Log it on successes too, so a complaint about a *bad answer* on a 200 is traceable. Keep your own correlation id as well and log the pair — your id joins the call to your trace, Anthropic's id lets support find it on their side. Neither replaces the other.",
w:"A — the header is available on normal responses through documented accessors; debug logging is a diagnostic aid, not the access mechanism. B — `error.message` is human-facing prose and is not a stable place to parse identifiers from; the body exposes `request_id` as its own field. C — a client-generated id is useful for your own tracing but is meaningless to Anthropic support, who need the server-issued value."},

{id:1187,d:"D1",s:"Error Handling & Retries",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"During a 20-minute platform incident, an ingestion service retries every failed call with exponential backoff and no attempt cap. Memory climbs steadily, the in-flight set reaches six figures, and the process is OOM-killed twice before the API recovers. Which control was missing?",
o:["A. A shorter per-attempt timeout, so failing calls release memory sooner","B. A larger backoff ceiling, so retries are spaced further apart and fewer are held","C. Streaming instead of buffered responses, so partial results are not accumulated","D. A bounded retry budget — a maximum attempt count and elapsed-time cap per item, with exhausted items moved to a dead-letter queue"],a:3,
e:"Unbounded retries convert a **dependency outage into a resource-exhaustion outage of your own service**. Every retrying item holds memory (request payload, accumulated history, closures) for the entire outage, so the in-flight set grows for as long as the incident lasts. The control is a **retry budget**: a hard attempt count *and* an elapsed-time cap per item, after which the item is durably parked — a **dead-letter queue** — and the resources freed. Combine with a circuit breaker so items are not even attempted while the dependency is known-down. Retries must be **bounded in both dimensions**.",
w:"A — a shorter timeout frees each attempt faster but the item is retained across all its attempts, so unbounded retrying still grows the set without limit. B — wider spacing keeps items alive *longer*, which increases peak retention rather than reducing it. C — streaming changes how a single response is consumed; it does not bound how many failed items are held in memory."},

{id:1188,d:"D1",s:"Error Handling & Retries",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"Which set of failures do the official SDKs retry automatically by default?",
o:["A. Every 4xx and 5xx response, on the assumption that any failure may be transient","B. Connection errors, 408 request timeouts, 409 conflicts, 429 rate limits, and status codes of 500 or above","C. Only 429 rate limits, because they alone carry a `retry-after` header","D. Only 500-class server errors; client-side connection failures are surfaced immediately"],a:1,
e:"The default list is deliberately **transient-only**: connection errors (network problems), **408** request timeout, **409** conflict, **429** rate limit, and **≥500** internal errors. Deterministic client faults — 400, 401, 402, 403, 404, 413 — are surfaced immediately, because no later attempt could change them. The count is **two retries by default** with short exponential backoff, honouring `retry-after` where present, and it is configurable via the max-retries option per client or per request. Knowing this list prevents both redundant wrappers and unnecessary hand-rolled logic.",
w:"A — retrying deterministic 4xx wastes time and load and masks configuration defects; the SDKs deliberately exclude them. C — 429 is retried, but so are connection errors, timeouts, conflicts and 5xx, none of which necessarily carry `retry-after`. D — connection errors are among the most retry-worthy failures and are retried by default."},

{id:1189,d:"D1",s:"Error Handling & Retries",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"An orchestration service manages long-lived API resources. One operation returns 409 `conflict_error` with a message about the resource having been modified concurrently. Two workers are known to touch the same resource. What is the appropriate handling?",
o:["A. Resolve the conflict — re-read current state, or de-duplicate the value that must be unique — then retry the request","B. Escalate to support with the request ID, since a conflict indicates server-side state corruption","C. Treat it as a permanent client defect and fail the operation without retrying, like a 400","D. Retry immediately in a tight loop until one worker wins, since exactly one attempt must eventually succeed"],a:0,
e:"**409 `conflict_error`** means the request conflicts with the resource's current state — concurrent modification, or a value that must be unique already in use. Unlike a 400, it is **conditionally retryable**: the *request* is well-formed, and the *world* is what disagrees with it. So a blind retry of the identical request may fail identically, while a retry after re-reading state can succeed. Note the SDKs include 409 in their default retry set, which handles the benign race; genuine uniqueness collisions need application-level resolution. The deeper fix is usually to remove the concurrent writer.",
w:"C — the request is not malformed and a retry after resolving state can succeed, so failing permanently discards recoverable work. B — a conflict is normal concurrency semantics, not corruption; support cannot resolve which of your two workers should win. D — a tight loop with no state refresh and no backoff amplifies contention and re-sends the same conflicting value."},

{id:1190,d:"D1",s:"Error Handling & Retries",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"An organisation's limit is 60 requests per minute. A scheduler fires all 60 calls in the first second of each minute and expects to sit exactly at the limit. Instead it takes 429s on most calls, then idles for 59 seconds. What explains this?",
o:["A. The limit is enforced against a rolling 60-second window, so the previous minute's calls are still counted","B. Rate limits are enforced at the workspace level in addition to the organisation level, halving the effective allowance","C. Requests are counted at response time rather than request time, so concurrent calls are all counted in the same instant","D. Limits are enforced with a token-bucket algorithm whose capacity replenishes continuously, so a 60 RPM allowance behaves closer to 1 request per second and an instantaneous burst of 60 exceeds available capacity"],a:3,
e:"The API uses a **token bucket**: capacity is **continuously replenished up to your maximum**, rather than being reset in full at fixed interval boundaries. The documented implication is explicit — a 60 RPM limit **may be enforced as roughly 1 request per second**, so short bursts can exceed the limit and trigger 429s even when your per-minute average is legally within allowance. The design consequence is to **pace requests evenly** through a bounded queue or client-side limiter rather than firing a per-minute batch, which also removes the 59 idle seconds and improves throughput.",
w:"A — the mechanism is continuous replenishment, not a rolling window ledger of prior calls. B — workspace limits exist and can be set lower than the organisation's, but nothing halves an allowance automatically. C — accounting timing is not the issue; the burst exceeds instantaneously available bucket capacity."},

{id:1191,d:"D1",s:"Error Handling & Retries",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A product launch takes a service from roughly 200 to 4,000 requests per minute overnight. It receives 429s carrying `retry-after`, even though the Console shows the organisation's tier limits are well above 4,000 RPM and token headroom is ample. What is the most likely cause and mitigation?",
o:["A. An acceleration limit triggered by the sharp increase in usage — ramp traffic up gradually and keep usage patterns consistent","B. The organisation has hit its monthly spend cap, which also returns 429","C. Requests are being counted against a stale limit until the next replenishment window","D. The tier upgrade has not propagated to the rate limiter and will apply after 24 hours"],a:0,
e:"Besides the published per-model limits, the API applies **acceleration limits**: a sharp, sudden increase in an organisation's usage can produce 429s even below the tier ceiling. The documented mitigation is to **ramp traffic gradually and maintain consistent usage patterns** — which for a launch means a staged rollout or a client-side ramp rather than flipping full traffic at once. The tell that separates this from a spend cap: an acceleration-limit 429 **carries `retry-after`**, whereas a tier spend-cap 429 has **no `retry-after`** and reports `error.details.error_code` as `enforced_spend_limit_reached`.",
w:"B — a spend-cap 429 arrives with no `retry-after` header and a distinguishing `error_code`, neither of which matches. C — the token bucket replenishes continuously against your current limit; there is no stale-limit window. D — nothing in the documented behaviour defers an applied tier change by a day; the symptom here is rate-of-change, not ceiling."},

{id:1192,d:"D1",s:"Error Handling & Retries",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A support assistant sends a 190,000-token knowledge base plus a short question on every turn and is constantly ITPM-throttled. After adding a `cache_control` breakpoint at the end of the knowledge base, `usage` shows large `cache_read_input_tokens` and small `input_tokens` — and the 429s largely stop, with no change to limits. Why did throttling improve?",
o:["A. Cached content is served from a regional edge that is exempt from organisation-level limits","B. On most models `cache_read_input_tokens` do not count toward the input-tokens-per-minute limit, so a high cache hit rate raises effective throughput at the same nominal limit","C. Caching reduces the request count, and ITPM is derived from RPM","D. `input_tokens` is the only field billed, so the reported total no longer reflects the true consumption"],a:1,
e:"For most models, **only uncached input tokens count toward ITPM**: `input_tokens` (the tokens after your last cache breakpoint) and `cache_creation_input_tokens` count, while **`cache_read_input_tokens` do not**. So an 80% hit rate against a 2M ITPM limit lets you push roughly 10M total input tokens per minute. Caching is therefore both a **cost** lever (reads bill at a fraction of base input) and a **rate-limit** lever. Two caveats: Claude Haiku 3.5 does count cache reads toward ITPM, and total input is `cache_read + cache_creation + input_tokens` — so `input_tokens` alone understates your request size.",
w:"A — limits are enforced at the organisation level regardless of where content is served from; there is no edge exemption. C — ITPM and RPM are independent limiters; caching did not reduce the number of requests here at all. D — cache reads are genuinely billed, at a reduced rate; the accounting is not fictional."},

{id:1193,d:"D1",s:"Error Handling & Retries",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"For most Claude models, which `usage` field does **not** count toward the input-tokens-per-minute rate limit?",
o:["A. `input_tokens`","B. `cache_creation_input_tokens`","C. `cache_read_input_tokens`","D. `output_tokens`"],a:2,
e:"ITPM counts **`input_tokens` + `cache_creation_input_tokens`**; **`cache_read_input_tokens` are excluded** on most models — a deliberate advantage that makes cached workloads far more throughput-efficient than the nominal limit suggests. Two things to hold alongside it: **Claude Haiku 3.5 is the documented exception** and does count cache reads toward ITPM; and `output_tokens` is governed by a **separate OTPM limiter**, not ITPM. Also remember `input_tokens` reports only the tokens *after* the last cache breakpoint, so total input is the sum of all three input fields.",
w:"A — `input_tokens` is uncached input and counts toward ITPM; it is the primary contributor when nothing is cached. B — writing to the cache counts toward ITPM, which is why the first request of a cached workload is the expensive one for throughput as well as cost. D — `output_tokens` is not part of ITPM at all; it is metered against the independent output-tokens-per-minute limit."},

{id:1194,d:"D1",s:"Error Handling & Retries",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A platform runs two workloads against one flagship model: latency-sensitive interactive chat, and a bulk enrichment job. Under load the enrichment job's volume 429s the chat traffic. The team routes enrichment to a smaller, cheaper model class and the chat 429s disappear immediately, with no limit increase. Why did that work?",
o:["A. Smaller models are exempt from rate limiting below a volume threshold","B. Smaller models consume fewer tokens per request, so total organisation-wide token consumption fell below the shared limit","C. Rate limits are applied separately for each model, so the two workloads now draw from independent buckets and can each run to their own limit","D. Routing to a second model class doubles the organisation's aggregate allowance for all models"],a:2,
e:"**Rate limits are applied separately per model**, so different models can be used up to their respective limits simultaneously. That makes **model routing a capacity-isolation tool**, not just a cost lever: putting a noisy bulk workload on a different model class stops it competing with interactive traffic for the same RPM/ITPM/OTPM buckets. Note the documented nuance — some related model versions share a *combined* bucket, so verify that your two targets are genuinely in different buckets before relying on the isolation. Priority Tier headers and the Batch API are complementary tools for the same problem.",
w:"A — there is no volume threshold below which a model class escapes rate limiting. B — token consumption per request may well have fallen, but the decisive change is that the limits are per model rather than one shared pool. D — no aggregate allowance is doubled; each model simply carries its own independent limits."},

{id:1195,d:"D1",s:"Error Handling & Retries",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"One team's workspace takes steady 429s while the Console shows the organisation as a whole using well under half its allowance. The organisation admin previously set a lower per-minute token limit on that workspace to protect other teams. What follows?",
o:["A. The workspace limit is the binding constraint, so the fix is a workspace-limit change (or lower demand) — organisation headroom is irrelevant to that workspace","B. Workspace limits are advisory and cannot cause 429s; the real cause must be an acceleration limit","C. Organisation limits always override workspace limits, so this configuration is impossible","D. Unused organisation capacity is automatically lent to a throttled workspace, so this will resolve itself"],a:0,
e:"Limits compose as a **nested pair of ceilings**, and the tightest one binds. Workspace limits are configurable **per limiter type** (requests, input tokens, output tokens), and where set they are enforced even if the organisation has headroom. The reverse also holds: **organisation-wide limits always apply**, even if the sum of workspace limits exceeds them. Two operational consequences — a 429 investigation must establish *which* ceiling was hit (the `anthropic-workspace-id` response header tells you which workspace a request counted against), and the generic `anthropic-ratelimit-tokens-*` headers report the **most restrictive** limit in effect, which will be the workspace one here.",
w:"B — workspace limits are enforced, not advisory, and an acceleration limit would not track a deliberately configured ceiling. C — organisation limits are an additional ceiling on top of workspace limits, not a replacement for them. D — unused capacity becomes available to *other* workspaces up to their own limits; it does not raise a workspace's configured ceiling."},

{id:1196,d:"D1",s:"Error Handling & Retries",t:"R",f:"H",
q:"A capacity-planning dashboard must display each workspace's **configured** rate limits, including for workspaces that have sent no traffic in the reporting window. What is the appropriate mechanism?",
o:["A. Read `anthropic-ratelimit-*-limit` headers from recent responses, which is the only programmatic source","B. Query the Rate Limits API, which returns the current organisation and workspace rate-limit configuration","C. Deliberately exceed each limit and parse the ceiling out of the resulting 429 message","D. Send a `count_tokens` request per workspace, which returns the applicable limits alongside `input_tokens`"],a:1,
e:"There are two distinct needs here. **Response headers** (`anthropic-ratelimit-*-limit` / `-remaining` / `-reset`) are the right tool for **live, per-request throttling decisions**, but they only exist where traffic exists. **Configuration** — what the limits *are*, for every workspace, traffic or not — is read from the **Rate Limits API**, the programmatic equivalent of the Console's Rate limits page. Use headers to steer the client in real time; use the API for planning, inventory and alerting on limit changes.",
w:"A — headers are excellent for live throttling but require traffic, so an idle workspace reports nothing and the dashboard has gaps. C — provoking 429s to discover ceilings inflicts real errors on real workloads and pollutes your reliability metrics. D — `count_tokens` returns exactly one field, `input_tokens`, and carries no limit configuration."},

{id:1197,d:"D1",s:"Error Handling & Retries",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A throttling client reads `anthropic-ratelimit-input-tokens-reset` and passes the value straight to a sleep helper that expects seconds. In staging the loop appears to hang indefinitely. What is wrong?",
o:["A. The header reports milliseconds, so the delay is a thousand times too long","B. The header is only present on 429 responses, so the client is sleeping on an undefined value","C. The reset headers report an absolute RFC 3339 timestamp of full replenishment, not a relative duration — it must be parsed and differenced against the current time","D. The value is rounded to the nearest thousand, and the rounding must be subtracted before use"],a:2,
e:"Every `anthropic-ratelimit-*-reset` header is an **absolute time in RFC 3339 format** — the moment the corresponding limit will be **fully replenished** — not a countdown. Interpreting a timestamp as a duration produces an astronomically long sleep. Parse it and subtract `now`. Contrast with **`retry-after` on a 429**, which *is* a relative number of seconds. Two headers, two units, and mixing them up is a classic outage. Note also that full replenishment is the *ceiling* of the wait: with a continuously-replenishing token bucket, partial capacity is usually available sooner.",
w:"A — the value is not a millisecond duration; it is a formatted timestamp string. B — rate-limit headers accompany successful responses too, which is what makes proactive throttling possible at all. D — the `-remaining` token headers are rounded to the nearest thousand; the `-reset` headers are timestamps and are not affected by that rounding."},

{id:1198,d:"D1",s:"Error Handling & Retries",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A retry helper reads `retry-after` and, when the header is absent, defaults to zero — retrying immediately. It runs correctly for a year. Then during a month-end billing lapse it enters a tight loop, issuing thousands of failing requests per minute. What went wrong and what is the correct default?",
o:["A. The `retry-after` header was stripped by an intermediary; the fix is to disable proxy header filtering","B. Retrying immediately is always correct when `retry-after` is absent, since absence signals capacity is available","C. The header only exists on 529s, so all 429s hit the zero default; the helper should hard-code a fixed delay for 429s","D. Some 429s — notably the tier spend-cap 429 — carry no `retry-after`; a missing header must fall back to jittered exponential backoff, and a non-retryable classification must stop the loop entirely"],a:3,
e:"Two defects compound. First, **`retry-after` is not guaranteed**: the documented spend-cap 429 deliberately omits it because **no retry will succeed until access resumes**. Second, defaulting a missing delay to **zero** turns any such response into an unbounded hot loop. Correct design: honour `retry-after` when present; otherwise fall back to **jittered exponential backoff with a floor**, never zero; and classify before retrying at all — a 429 whose `error.details.error_code` is `enforced_spend_limit_reached` should break the loop and page a human rather than back off.",
w:"A — the header's absence here is by design on this response, not a proxy artefact, so proxy configuration would not change it. B — absence carries no such meaning, and treating it as permission to retry instantly is precisely how a hot loop forms. C — `retry-after` is documented on rate-limit 429s; a blanket fixed delay also synchronises the fleet by removing jitter."},

{id:1199,d:"D1",s:"Error Handling & Retries",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A client computes a backoff of 2 seconds for its first retry. The 429 response carries `retry-after: 34`. Its policy is to \"use the smaller of the computed delay and the header, to stay responsive\". Throughput collapses and the 429 rate stays pinned. Why?",
o:["A. Retrying before the interval named in `retry-after` is guaranteed to fail, so 17 wasted attempts are made before the capacity is even available","B. The header is a recommendation for streaming requests only and does not apply to buffered calls","C. `retry-after` restarts the rate-limit window on each request, so early retries extend the throttle","D. The computed delay must be added to the header value, and taking a minimum halves the total wait"],a:0,
e:"`retry-after` states **how long to wait before you can retry — earlier retries will fail**. It is server-authoritative: it reflects the actual replenishment state of your bucket, which no client-side heuristic can know. Retrying at 2 s against a 34 s window buys nothing but 17 failed round trips, each adding load and each still 429. The rule is **honour `retry-after` as a floor, never a ceiling**: `wait = max(retry_after, jittered_backoff)`. Guessed delays are a fallback for when the header is absent, not a competitor to it. If a 34 s wait is unacceptable to the user, the answer is a queue or a degraded mode, not a shorter sleep.",
w:"B — the header applies to rate-limited responses generally and is honoured by the SDKs' own retry logic regardless of streaming. C — retrying early does not reset or extend the window; it simply fails. D — the header is an absolute minimum wait, not an increment to be summed with a local computation."},

{id:1200,d:"D1",s:"Error Handling & Retries",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A pipeline uses `/v1/messages/count_tokens` before every call and rejects anything above a token threshold, expecting this to prevent all size-related failures. It still receives occasional 413 `request_too_large` on requests that passed the check. Why is the guard insufficient?",
o:["A. `count_tokens` returns an estimate, and the small error margin is enough to cross the token limit","B. 413 is a limit on request **bytes**, which a token count cannot predict — base64 media inflates payload size far faster than token count","C. `count_tokens` does not include tool definitions, so their tokens are unaccounted for","D. The 413 threshold varies by model, and the pipeline is checking against the wrong model's ceiling"],a:1,
e:"**413 `request_too_large` is a byte-size ceiling on the HTTP request**, enforced per endpoint and, on the direct API, applied at the edge before the request reaches the API servers. Token count and byte count are only loosely related: a base64-encoded image or PDF adds enormous payload bytes relative to its visual-token cost, and JSON structure adds bytes with no tokens at all. So guard **both** dimensions — measure the serialised payload size as well as tokens — and for large media use the **Files API** and reference by `file_id` instead of inlining bytes.",
w:"A — `count_tokens` is indeed an estimate, but a percentage-level margin is not what turns a compliant request into a multi-megabyte one. C — `count_tokens` explicitly covers messages, `system` and `tools` in its `input_tokens` figure. D — the request-size ceiling is defined per endpoint rather than per model, so model selection is not the variable here."},

{id:1201,d:"D1",s:"Error Handling & Retries",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"Comparing the documented maximum request sizes across endpoints, which endpoint accepts the largest single request?",
o:["A. The Messages API","B. The Token Counting API","C. The Files API","D. The Message Batches API"],a:2,
e:"Request-size ceilings are **per endpoint**, and they rise with the endpoint's purpose: the Messages and Token Counting endpoints share the smallest ceiling, the Batch endpoint allows substantially more (it packs many requests into one submission), and the **Files API allows the largest single request** — it exists precisely to carry bulk bytes. Exceeding any of them returns **413 `request_too_large`**. The design lesson: when a document will not fit in a Messages request, the answer is to **upload it via the Files API and reference it by `file_id`**, not to compress the JSON.",
w:"A — the Messages endpoint has the tightest ceiling, which is why inlining large base64 media there is the most common source of 413. B — Token Counting shares the same modest ceiling as Messages, since it accepts the same shape of payload. D — the Batch endpoint's ceiling is larger than Messages but is not the largest; it is sized for many small requests rather than one large upload."},

{id:1202,d:"D1",s:"Error Handling & Retries",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A non-streaming call with a very large `max_tokens` intermittently fails on a corporate network with no error body — sometimes a connection reset, sometimes a client timeout, and once a 504 `timeout_error`. The same call succeeds from a laptop on a home connection. What is the correct engineering response?",
o:["A. Increase the SDK's max-retries option so the flaky network is absorbed by additional attempts","B. Reduce `max_tokens` until responses fit inside the network's idle-connection window","C. Move the call behind a synchronous HTTP proxy with a longer keep-alive so the buffered response arrives intact","D. Switch to the streaming Messages API (or the Batch API for offline work), since long non-streaming requests are vulnerable to idle-connection drops and network variance"],a:3,
e:"A long non-streaming request holds an **idle connection** for the entire generation, and many networks drop idle connections after a variable period — producing resets, client timeouts, or a **504 `timeout_error`**. The documented guidance for long-running work, especially anything over about ten minutes, is **streaming** (bytes flow continuously, so the connection is never idle, and it removes the SDK's pre-flight objection to over-long non-streaming calls) or the **Batch API** (poll for results, so no uninterrupted connection is needed at all). TCP keep-alive helps, and the SDKs already set it, but it does not make a buffered multi-minute call robust.",
w:"A — retrying re-runs a full expensive generation and remains equally exposed to the same idle-connection behaviour on each attempt. B — shrinking the ceiling truncates legitimate output with `stop_reason: \"max_tokens\"` and only shortens the exposure window rather than closing it. C — a proxy in the path adds another component that can buffer or drop the connection; it does not change the fact that the connection sits idle."},

{id:1203,d:"D1",s:"Error Handling & Retries",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"A call returns `{\"type\":\"error\",\"error\":{\"type\":\"api_error\",...},\"request_id\":\"req_01...\"}` with HTTP 500. What is the documented handling?",
o:["A. Retry with exponential backoff, and if the error persists contact support with the request ID","B. Do not retry; a 500 means the request itself is invalid and must be corrected","C. Retry immediately without delay, since a 500 is a transient internal condition","D. Fall back to a different model, since a 500 indicates the requested model is unavailable"],a:0,
e:"**500 `api_error`** is an unexpected error internal to Anthropic's systems — nothing about your request caused it, so it belongs in the **retryable** bucket. The documented handling is **exponential backoff** (with jitter, so a fleet does not resynchronise) and, if it persists, **escalation to support carrying the `request_id`** from the error body or the `request-id` header. That is exactly why logging the identifier on every failure pays for itself. The SDKs already retry ≥500 twice by default. Distinguish it from 529 `overloaded_error`, which signals platform-wide load rather than an unexpected internal fault.",
w:"B — request-validity problems are 400 `invalid_request_error`; a 500 originates on the server side. C — retrying with no delay risks hammering a system that is already failing and synchronises the fleet; backoff is explicitly specified. D — a 500 says nothing about model availability, and switching models hides a fault that support should be told about."},

{id:1204,d:"D1",s:"Error Handling & Retries",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"An agent loop has completed six iterations, executing a `create_ticket` tool on iteration 3 and a `send_email` tool on iteration 5. Iteration 7 returns 529. The retry wrapper wraps the whole loop, so it restarts from iteration 1. Users receive duplicate tickets and emails. What is the design error?",
o:["A. The 529 should not have been retried at all, since platform overload requires a circuit breaker rather than a retry","B. Retry scope was placed around the whole stateful loop instead of the single failed API call, so replaying the loop re-executes side effects that already succeeded","C. The accumulated conversation history should have been discarded before retrying, which would have prevented duplicate tool calls","D. Tool results should have been sent as a `tool` role message, which would have made the replay idempotent"],a:1,
e:"**Retry scope must match the unit of failure.** What failed was one HTTP call; the completed tool executions are durable facts. Retrying the *call* — resending the same accumulated history, which the stateless API requires anyway — resumes exactly where it stopped and repeats nothing. Retrying the *loop* replays every side effect. Two layered defences: keep the retry at the API-call boundary, and make side-effecting tools **idempotent** with a stable key per logical operation, so that even a mis-scoped retry or an ambiguous timeout cannot double-charge. The narrower the retry scope, the less state has to be idempotent.",
w:"A — 529 is transient and genuinely warrants backoff; a breaker complements retries for sustained outages but is not what caused the duplicates. C — discarding history would lose the tool results the model needs and force it to call the tools again, making duplication worse. D — there is no `tool` role: results go in a `user` message as `tool_result` blocks, and message shape has no bearing on side-effect idempotency."},

{id:1205,d:"D1",s:"Error Handling & Retries",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"Which approach to error handling with an official SDK is recommended?",
o:["A. Catch the base error class only, then inspect `error.message` with regular expressions to decide the branch","B. Compare the numeric status code against a hard-coded table maintained in application code","C. Catch the SDK's typed exception classes — handling the most specific classes before more general ones — rather than string-matching messages","D. Let exceptions propagate uncaught and rely on the process supervisor to restart the worker"],a:2,
e:"The SDKs raise **typed exceptions** rather than returning raw JSON, so the error class *is* the classification: a 429 surfaces as a rate-limit error type, a 404 as a not-found type, connection failures as their own class, and ≥500 as an internal-server type. Class names differ per language, and one SDK (Go) exposes a single error value you branch on by status. Two rules follow: **catch the specific classes before the general ones**, since they inherit from a common base and a broad catch placed first swallows everything; and **never branch on message text**, which is human-facing prose outside the versioning contract.",
w:"A — messages can be reworded at any time, so regular-expression branching is fragile by construction and the typed classes already encode what you need. B — a hand-maintained status table drifts as the documented type set expands, and it discards the SDK's connection-error class which has no status code at all. D — restarting on every rate limit or transient 5xx converts a recoverable condition into an outage and loses in-flight work."},

{id:1206,d:"D1",s:"Error Handling & Retries",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"An asynchronous document-classification worker exhausts its retry budget on about 40 items a day — some 400s from malformed source files, some persistent 429s during peak. Today the code logs the failure and drops the item. Compliance asks how many documents were never classified and the team cannot answer. What is the missing component?",
o:["A. A longer retry budget, so items eventually succeed and the question becomes moot","B. Structured logging at WARN level with the `request_id`, which is sufficient to reconstruct the set from logs","C. A metrics counter per `error.type`, so the daily total is visible on a dashboard","D. A dead-letter queue that durably stores each exhausted item with its payload, final `error.type` and `request_id`, so failures can be counted, inspected and replayed"],a:3,
e:"Logs and counters answer *how many* and *why*; they do not let you **replay**. A **dead-letter queue** makes an exhausted item a first-class durable record — payload, attempt history, final `error.type`, `request_id` — which delivers three things a log line cannot: an authoritative count for audit, a corpus to triage (the 400s are a code or data defect to fix; the 429s are capacity to re-drive), and **re-processing after the fix** without re-running the whole corpus. Transferable principle: **a bounded retry budget requires somewhere for the work to go when the budget is spent**, or bounding it silently loses data.",
w:"A — some of these failures are deterministic 400s that no budget can resolve, and unbounded retrying reintroduces resource exhaustion. B — logs are typically sampled, retained briefly and not designed for replay; reconstructing payloads from them is unreliable. C — a counter gives the number but not the items, so nothing can be inspected or re-processed."},

{id:1207,d:"D1",s:"Error Handling & Retries",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"An on-call team wants an alert that fires on genuine integration breakage without paging during normal capacity pressure. Which alert definition best achieves that?",
o:["A. Alert when the count of deterministic 4xx (400/401/403/404/413) exceeds a sustained rate threshold, with 429 and 529 tracked on separate, lower-urgency signals","B. Alert when total API error count crosses a fixed threshold, regardless of type","C. Alert only when a single request fails after all retries are exhausted, whatever the cause","D. Alert when p99 latency rises, since every error class ultimately shows up as latency"],a:0,
e:"The two populations differ in **who fixes them and how fast**. Deterministic 4xx means *your requests are wrong* — a schema change, rotated secret, revoked permission, oversized payload — it will not self-heal, and it usually correlates with a deploy, so it should page the owning team. **429 and 529 are capacity events** that your backoff, queueing and circuit breaker are designed to absorb; they warrant lower-urgency signals and escalate only on sustained duration or budget exhaustion. Alert on **rate rather than raw count** so the threshold survives traffic growth, and always break out by `error.type`.",
w:"B — a blended count fires on ordinary peak-hour 429s and stays silent when a low-volume endpoint breaks completely. C — post-retry exhaustion is a good signal for capacity events but is far too late for a deterministic 4xx, which is known bad on the first attempt. D — latency is a symptom that lags and blurs the classes; a fast-failing 401 storm may not move p99 at all."},

{id:1208,d:"D1",s:"Error Handling & Retries",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A scheduled key rotation replaces the API key in the secret store, but one long-running worker process holds the old value in memory from startup. It begins returning 401 `authentication_error` on every call while its siblings are healthy. The retry layer backs off up to 5 minutes per call. What should happen?",
o:["A. Retries should continue, since the worker will pick up the new secret when its backoff exceeds the secret cache interval","B. 401 should be classified non-retryable so the worker fails fast, and the process should reload the credential (or be restarted) as the actual remedy","C. The worker should fall back to a second model class, since 401 can indicate model-level entitlement loss","D. The organisation should re-issue the key, since a 401 on one worker means the rotation itself failed"],a:1,
e:"A 401 is **deterministic for a given credential** — a process holding a stale key will 401 forever, and backoff merely converts a fast, loud failure into a slow, quiet one that inflates latency and delays detection. Correct handling has two parts: **classify 401 as non-retryable** so it surfaces immediately and pages, and **fix the cause** by re-reading the secret at the point of use or on a refresh signal rather than caching it for the process lifetime. Note the contrast with 403 `permission_error`, which arrives on a *valid* credential lacking authorisation — also non-retryable, but a different remedy.",
w:"A — nothing about backoff triggers a secret reload; the process will keep presenting the same stale key indefinitely. C — model entitlement problems surface as 403 `permission_error` or a 400, not 401, and switching models with a bad credential fails identically. D — the siblings are healthy, which proves the new key works; the defect is local credential caching."},

{id:1209,d:"D1",s:"Error Handling & Retries",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"A request returns 403 `permission_error`. Which investigation is indicated?",
o:["A. Validate the JSON body against the Messages API schema, since 403 signals a structural violation","B. Retry with exponential backoff, since 403 is issued when a resource is temporarily locked","C. Check the organisation's access settings and workspace configuration, because the key authenticated but is not authorised for the specified resource","D. Rotate the API key, since 403 means the credential has expired"],a:2,
e:"**403 `permission_error`** means the API key **does not have permission to use the specified resource** — authentication succeeded, authorisation did not. The investigation is therefore entirely in the Console: organisation access settings and workspace configuration. Hold the 401/403 boundary firmly, because the remedies are unrelated: **401 `authentication_error`** is a bad credential (malformed, revoked, expired) and is fixed by re-issuing or redeploying a secret; **403** is a good credential pointed at something it may not touch, and is fixed by changing a grant. Both are deterministic and neither should be retried.",
w:"A — a malformed or invalid body is 400 `invalid_request_error`; the request here parsed fine. B — 403 is a stable authorisation verdict, not a transient lock, so retrying reproduces it indefinitely. D — an expired or revoked key returns 401 `authentication_error`; rotating a working key changes nothing."},

{id:1210,d:"D1",s:"Error Handling & Retries",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"An extraction service reports a 6% \"API failure rate\". Digging in, the failures are HTTP 200 responses containing a `tool_use` block whose accumulated arguments fail the team's own JSON validation. Their retry layer re-issues the identical request up to three times; roughly a third of retries produce valid arguments. What is the correct diagnosis and fix?",
o:["A. It is a transport fault caused by `input_json_delta` fragments arriving out of order; the fix is to sort deltas by `index` before parsing","B. It is a rate-limit symptom; adding backoff will raise the success rate further","C. It is a 500-class fault being masked by the SDK's retries and should be escalated to support with the request IDs","D. It is a model-content problem, not an API failure; the fix is constrained decoding — `strict: true` on the tool, or `output_config.format` with a JSON schema — with validation and a bounded repair path behind it"],a:3,
e:"A 200 is a successful API call. Output that fails *your* schema is a **model-content problem**, and the tell is right there in the data: identical retries sometimes succeed, which means the variance is in generation, not transport. Blind retrying is the expensive workaround — full cost, non-deterministic, and it inflates a metric that then misdirects on-call. The current mechanism is **constrained decoding**: `strict: true` on the tool definition, or `output_config.format` with `{\"type\":\"json_schema\", ...}` for the response body, which guarantees schema-valid output with no beta header. Keep validation as a backstop. This **supersedes** the legacy \"force a schema-bearing tool and retry on parse failure\" pattern.",
w:"A — every streaming block event carries an `index` and `partial_json` fragments must simply be concatenated per index before parsing; that is a real client bug class, but it would not be intermittently fixed by an identical retry. B — no 429 is involved; these calls returned 200 and consumed full quota. C — nothing here is a 5xx, and support cannot act on successful requests whose content did not match a private schema."},

{id:1211,d:"D1",s:"Error Handling & Retries",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A retry classifier treats \"result unusable\" as retryable. It therefore retries any response whose `stop_reason` is `max_tokens`, sending the identical request. Cost doubles on affected calls and the output truncates at exactly the same point every time. What is the classifier's mistake?",
o:["A. Truncation is deterministic given identical input and an identical `max_tokens`, so it is a configuration or prompting problem, not a transient failure","B. `max_tokens` responses must be retried with a `stop_sequence` added, which the classifier omitted","C. `stop_reason: \"max_tokens\"` indicates a rate-limit truncation and should be retried only after `retry-after` elapses","D. The retry is correct in principle but must use a different model, since truncation is model-specific"],a:0,
e:"`stop_reason: \"max_tokens\"` on a 200 means the generation hit **your configured output ceiling**. Nothing transient is involved: the same request with the same ceiling truncates in the same place, so a retry buys a second full-price truncation. The remedies are all **deliberate changes** — raise `max_tokens` (subject to the model's ceiling; above it you get a 400, with no silent clamping), ask for shorter output, or chunk the task. Transferable principle: **a retry is only justified when a later attempt could plausibly differ**. Truncation, refusals and schema mismatches are outcomes to design around, not faults to re-roll.",
w:"B — a stop sequence causes the model to stop *earlier*, which makes truncation worse rather than better. C — rate limits are HTTP 429 responses; `max_tokens` is a normal, successful, fully-billed 200. D — a different model has a different output ceiling and different verbosity, but the classifier's error is calling a deterministic outcome transient."},

{id:1212,d:"D1",s:"Error Handling & Retries",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A team wants a self-tuning concurrency ceiling that keeps utilisation high without provoking 429s. Which control loop is soundest?",
o:["A. Fix concurrency at the value that produced peak throughput during a one-off load test, and treat 429s as noise thereafter","B. Read the dimension-specific `anthropic-ratelimit-*-remaining` headers on every response, reduce in-flight concurrency as the tightest dimension approaches exhaustion, and increase it cautiously while all dimensions hold headroom","C. Set concurrency to the organisation's RPM limit divided by sixty, and hold it constant","D. Run unbounded concurrency and derive the ceiling empirically from the observed 429 rate"],a:1,
e:"Proactive throttling means treating the rate-limit headers as a **feedback signal**, not post-mortem data. Read `-remaining` for **requests**, **input tokens** and **output tokens** separately — they deplete at different rates depending on workload shape — and let the **tightest** dimension govern. Decrease quickly on pressure, increase slowly when clear (an additive-increase / multiplicative-decrease shape), so the loop is stable rather than oscillating. Combine with `retry-after` on the 429s you do take, and remember these headers are visible on **successful** responses too, which is what makes prevention possible at all.",
w:"A — a static value tuned once drifts as prompt sizes, cache hit rates, model routing and limits change, and ignoring 429s discards the only corrective signal. C — that arithmetic assumes every call takes exactly one second and ignores the token dimensions entirely, which usually bind first. D — discovering the ceiling by failing means paying in errors and retry load continuously, and the token bucket means bursts fail even within a legal average."},

{id:1213,d:"D1",s:"Error Handling & Retries",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"An organisation sets its own spend limit in the Console, below its tier's cap. When usage reaches that self-imposed limit, what does the API return?",
o:["A. 429 `rate_limit_error` with a `retry-after` header set to the seconds remaining in the month","B. 402 `billing_error`, since the condition concerns spend","C. 400 `invalid_request_error`, with a message beginning that the specified API usage limits have been reached","D. 403 `permission_error`, because the key is no longer authorised to incur spend"],a:2,
e:"There are three distinct spend-related outcomes and mixing them up is a common triage failure. A **limit you configured** — organisation or workspace — returns **400 `invalid_request_error`**, with a message beginning \"You have reached your specified API usage limits\" (or the workspace variant). A **usage-tier monthly cap** returns **429 `rate_limit_error`** with no `retry-after` and `error.details.error_code` of `enforced_spend_limit_reached`. A **payment problem** returns **402 `billing_error`**. All three are non-retryable and need a human action, so branch on type *and* read the message. One documented exception: limits on the Claude Code workspace can return a 429 carrying `retry-after`.",
w:"A — that shape belongs to the usage-tier cap, and even there no `retry-after` is sent. B — 402 is reserved for problems with billing or payment information, not for a deliberately configured ceiling. D — 403 concerns resource authorisation for a key; a spend ceiling is not modelled as a permission."},

{id:1214,d:"D1",s:"Error Handling & Retries",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A product owner requires that a summarisation feature \"never show an error page\". Traffic is bursty and the team already sees 429s at peak. Which degradation ladder is the soundest design?",
o:["A. Retry indefinitely with exponential backoff and hold the request open until it eventually succeeds","B. Silently return a generic placeholder summary whenever the API fails, so no error is ever surfaced","C. Route all failures to a smaller model class, which has no rate limits and therefore cannot fail","D. Serve a cached or precomputed summary if one exists; otherwise queue the work and return an explicit \"in progress\" state; escalate to a visible error only when the queue itself is unavailable"],a:3,
e:"\"Never show an error\" is a requirement about **user experience**, not about the API becoming infallible — so it is met with a **layered fallback**, each layer cheaper and more available than the last: cached or precomputed result → asynchronous queue with an honest \"working on it\" state → last-resort visible failure. Two non-negotiables: every layer must **bound its wait** (holding a request open is an outage in disguise), and degraded output must be **labelled** rather than silently substituted, or users act on stale or generic content believing it fresh. Pair with a circuit breaker so the ladder is entered fast under sustained 429/529 instead of after a full backoff.",
w:"A — an unbounded hold exhausts connections and threads and shows the user an indefinite spinner, which is a worse failure than an error page. B — passing off a placeholder as a real summary is a silent-correctness failure and destroys trust once discovered. C — every model class carries its own rate limits, so a smaller model is a useful capacity-isolation step but not an immune one."},

{id:1215,d:"D1",s:"Error Handling & Retries",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"During an incident, the team cannot tell whether elevated errors are their own deploy or a platform problem. Which lightweight signal would have answered that in seconds?",
o:["A. A canary that issues a small, fixed, known-good request on a schedule from a separate code path, recording status, `error.type` and `request-id`","B. A larger sample of production request bodies logged at DEBUG for offline analysis","C. An alert on total error count with a lower threshold, so the incident is caught earlier","D. A second API key used only during incidents, to rule out credential problems"],a:0,
e:"A **synthetic canary** isolates the variable. Because its payload is fixed and known-good and it bypasses the application's own request-building code, its result is unambiguous: canary failing **and** production failing points at the platform (expect 529 `overloaded_error` or 500 `api_error`); canary healthy **while** production fails points squarely at your deploy — a malformed body, a rotated secret, a deprecated parameter. Recording `error.type` and `request-id` on every canary run also gives support a clean reproduction. Cheap to build, and it converts the most expensive minutes of an incident into a glance.",
w:"B — body logs help root-cause a specific 400 afterwards but do not tell you, during the incident, whether the API itself is healthy. C — a lower threshold changes when you learn there is a problem, not which side of the boundary it is on. D — a second key tests only credentials, and organisation-level rate limits and platform overload affect both keys identically."},

{id:1216,d:"D1",s:"Error Handling & Retries",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"Twelve independent worker processes each implement correct jittered exponential backoff. Collectively they still oscillate: long 429 bursts alternating with periods of low utilisation. Each worker's own logs look textbook. What is the structural problem?",
o:["A. Jitter windows must be identical across workers to be effective, and each process is randomising differently","B. Every worker should honour `retry-after` from the most recent 429 seen by any worker, which requires no shared state","C. Rate limits are enforced per organisation, so twelve independent limiters cannot know the aggregate — a shared limiter (a distributed token bucket or a central queue) is needed to pace total demand","D. Backoff should be replaced by a fixed delay so all twelve workers converge on a stable, predictable rate"],a:2,
e:"Per-process backoff is **locally correct and globally blind**. The allowance is shared at the **organisation level**, so each worker's view of \"how hard am I pushing?\" is one twelfth of the truth. Each backs off on its own errors, all recover independently, and the aggregate overshoots — hence the oscillation. The fix is **shared flow control**: a distributed token bucket in Redis, or a single queue with a global concurrency ceiling, so total demand is paced against total allowance. Keep per-request jittered backoff underneath as a second line of defence. Adding more API keys does not help, since limits are per organisation.",
w:"A — identical jitter windows would re-correlate the workers, which is the opposite of what jitter is for; independent randomisation is correct. B — sharing only the last `retry-after` propagates a stale, request-specific value and still leaves total demand unmanaged. D — a fixed delay removes jitter and synchronises all twelve workers into waves, worsening the oscillation."},

{id:1217,d:"D1",s:"Error Handling & Retries",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"Which expression best represents a well-formed retry delay for a transient API failure on attempt `n`?",
o:["A. `base * n`","B. `min(cap, base * 2^n)`","C. `random(0, min(cap, base * 2^n))`","D. `cap - (base * n)`"],a:2,
e:"Three properties are needed and only one option has all of them. **Exponential growth** (`base * 2^n`) so repeated failure reduces pressure fast; a **ceiling** (`min(cap, ...)`) so the delay cannot grow absurd on a long outage; and **jitter** (the random draw) so independent clients de-correlate instead of retrying in synchronised waves. This particular form is \"full jitter\". Two additions in production: honour `retry-after` as a **floor** — `wait = max(retry_after, jittered)` — since the server knows your bucket state better than your formula does, and bound total attempts and elapsed time so retries cannot run forever.",
w:"A — linear growth applies too much pressure for too long on a struggling dependency and, being deterministic, keeps a failed fleet in lockstep. B — correct growth and a correct ceiling, but with no randomisation every client that failed together retries together; this is the thundering-herd formula. D — a delay that shrinks as failures accumulate accelerates pressure exactly when the dependency is least able to absorb it, and can go negative."},

{id:1218,d:"D1",s:"Error Handling & Retries",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"A team compares three retry-delay strategies for a fleet of clients sharing one rate-limit allowance: no jitter, full jitter (`random(0, d)`), and equal jitter (`d/2 + random(0, d/2)`). What is the correct trade-off summary?",
o:["A. No jitter is preferable when the fleet is large, because predictable timing makes capacity planning possible","B. Full jitter and equal jitter are equivalent, since both draw from a random distribution","C. Equal jitter is unsafe because its minimum delay is non-zero, so a client can wait past the point at which capacity returned","D. No jitter synchronises the fleet into waves; full jitter spreads retries most widely but allows very short delays; equal jitter guarantees a minimum wait while still de-correlating"],a:3,
e:"All three share the same exponential backbone; they differ in the **distribution of arrivals**. **No jitter** is the failure case — clients that failed together retry together, so each tier re-saturates the allowance. **Full jitter** spreads arrivals over the widest interval and disperses a herd fastest, at the cost of occasionally drawing a near-zero delay. **Equal jitter** trades some of that spread for a guaranteed minimum wait, which suits a dependency that needs recovery time. Either randomised form is far better than none, and both should still take `retry-after` as a floor and sit under a bounded attempt budget.",
w:"A — predictability is precisely the defect at fleet scale; synchronised retries produce the spikes that keep everyone throttled. B — the distributions differ materially: one can return almost immediately, the other enforces a floor, which changes recovery behaviour. C — a guaranteed minimum wait is a deliberate, useful property, not a safety problem; slightly late is far cheaper than a synchronised stampede."},

{id:1219,d:"D1",s:"Error Handling & Retries",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"To work around persistent 429s, a team provisions four additional API keys in the same organisation and round-robins traffic across all five. The 429 rate is unchanged. What did they misunderstand, and what would actually help?",
o:["A. Rate limits are enforced at the organisation level, so extra keys share the same buckets; real relief comes from shaping demand, isolating workloads across model classes, caching, or requesting higher limits","B. Each key needs its own `anthropic-version` header before it receives an independent allowance","C. Round-robin defeats the token bucket's replenishment; sticky assignment of keys to workers would have worked","D. The keys must be placed in separate workspaces, which grants each an allowance additional to the organisation's"],a:0,
e:"Limits are set and enforced **at the organisation level**, so multiplying credentials multiplies nothing — and it costs you observability, since traffic is now smeared across five keys. Workspace limits can be set *lower* to protect other teams, but **organisation-wide limits always apply even if workspace limits sum to more**, so they cannot be used to manufacture extra capacity. Genuine levers: pace demand with a shared limiter, exploit that limits are **per model** to isolate bulk work onto another model class, raise effective ITPM with **prompt caching** (cache reads do not count on most models), move offline volume to the **Batch API**, and request a limit increase.",
w:"B — `anthropic-version` is a required header on every request and has no bearing on allowance allocation. C — the token bucket replenishes continuously and is accounted at the organisation level; the distribution of requests across keys is irrelevant to it. D — workspaces exist to set *lower* ceilings, and the organisation ceiling still binds regardless of how workspace limits add up."},

{id:1220,d:"D1",s:"Error Handling & Retries",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A service mixes interactive requests with a large nightly re-classification of archived records. During the overlap window both take 429s and interactive latency suffers. Deadlines for the archive work are measured in days. What is the most appropriate structural change?",
o:["A. Give interactive traffic a shorter retry delay so it wins contention against the archive job","B. Move the archive workload to the Message Batches API, which has its own rate limits, roughly halves cost, and returns results within 24 hours","C. Raise `max_tokens` on interactive calls so they complete in fewer round trips","D. Run the archive job with retries disabled, so its failures do not add load"],a:1,
e:"This is a **workload-class** problem, not a retry-tuning problem. The Batch API is built for exactly this shape: **no deadline pressure**, high volume. It carries **its own rate limits, separate from the Messages API**, so bulk work stops competing with interactive traffic for the same RPM/ITPM/OTPM buckets; it costs roughly **50% less**; and it completes **within 24 hours**. Two practical notes: give each request a **`custom_id`**, since results are returned as JSONL with **no order guarantee**, and prefer the **1-hour cache TTL** for batches because the 5-minute default can expire mid-batch.",
w:"A — shorter delays for interactive traffic add load during contention and remove the jitter that keeps the fleet dispersed; it does not reduce the archive job's demand. C — `max_tokens` is an output ceiling, does not factor into output-token rate limits, and does not reduce round trips. D — disabling retries throws away recoverable work and leaves the underlying contention entirely intact."},

{id:1221,d:"D1",s:"Error Handling & Retries",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"A request includes a field name that the Messages API does not recognise. Which status and `error.type` results, and what else does that pairing cover?",
o:["A. 422 `unprocessable_entity_error`, which covers all schema-level violations","B. 409 `conflict_error`, which covers requests that disagree with server state","C. 400 `invalid_request_error`, which covers problems with the format or content of the request and also absorbs other 4xx conditions not separately documented","D. 500 `api_error`, because an unrecognised field cannot be parsed by the server"],a:2,
e:"**400 `invalid_request_error`** is the general-purpose client-fault code: anything wrong with the **format or content** of the request, and the docs note it **may also be used for other 4XX status codes not separately listed**. Its documented population is worth memorising because it is broad — unknown or malformed fields, an unmatched `tool_use_id`, `max_tokens` above the model's ceiling, a 5th cache breakpoint, an invalid or unentitled `anthropic-beta` value, deprecated `temperature`/`top_p`/`top_k`, assistant prefill on current models, `thinking: {\"type\":\"enabled\"}` on 4.7+, and an organisation or workspace spend limit you configured. All deterministic; none retryable.",
w:"A — 422 appears in SDK exception tables as a mapped class but is not the documented type for a malformed Messages request body. B — 409 `conflict_error` concerns a clash with the current state of a resource, not request syntax. D — a 500 `api_error` is an unexpected internal Anthropic fault; a rejected field is a validated client error."},

{id:1222,d:"D1",s:"Error Handling & Retries",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A user reports a bad answer at 14:32 and quotes a support reference. The team must find the exact API call behind it across three services and a fan-out step. Which logging design makes this a single query?",
o:["A. Log the full request and response bodies for every call, so any answer can be located by text search","B. Log the `error.type` and status code for every call, indexed by timestamp","C. Log the model id and token `usage` per call, since together they uniquely identify a generation","D. Log Anthropic's `request-id` alongside your own trace or correlation id, user id and logical operation id, on successes as well as failures"],a:3,
e:"Two identifiers, two jobs, and you need both **joined on the same line**. Your **trace or correlation id** stitches the call into your own distributed trace — which service, which user, which fan-out branch. Anthropic's **`request-id`** (also the top-level `request_id` in error bodies) is the only handle **support** can act on. Logging the pair on every call — crucially including **200s**, since a bad answer arrives on a successful response — turns \"find the call behind this complaint\" into one indexed lookup. The SDKs expose the value directly (`_request_id`, raw-response accessors, or middleware), so this costs almost nothing to add.",
w:"A — full-body logging is expensive, expands the sensitive-data surface, and text search still cannot reliably disambiguate similar generations. B — status and type describe failures; the reported problem is a successful response with poor content, which this scheme does not even record. C — model id and token counts are not unique per call; thousands of calls share them."},

{id:1223,d:"D1",s:"Error Handling & Retries",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"A request returns **504** with `error.type` of `timeout_error`. What does the documentation recommend?",
o:["A. Consider the streaming Messages API for long-running requests, or the Message Batches API","B. Treat it as a client-side defect and validate the request body","C. Retry immediately on the same connection, since the request is still queued server-side","D. Lower `max_tokens` until the response fits within the fixed server-side time budget"],a:0,
e:"**504 `timeout_error`** means the request timed out while being processed. The documented direction is architectural rather than a retry tweak: use the **streaming Messages API** for long-running requests — bytes flow continuously, so nothing sits idle and the client can render progressively — or the **Message Batches API**, where you poll for results and need no uninterrupted connection at all. This is the same guidance that governs long requests generally, and it is why the SDKs object to non-streaming calls expected to exceed roughly ten minutes.",
w:"B — a malformed body is 400 `invalid_request_error`; a 504 arrives on a request that was accepted and processed. C — the connection has ended and the work is not held in a resumable queue; an immediate identical retry is likely to time out the same way. D — shrinking the output ceiling truncates legitimate responses and treats a structural mismatch as a parameter problem."},

{id:1224,d:"D1",s:"Error Handling & Retries",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A client sets a 30-second timeout. A request that genuinely takes 45 seconds is abandoned client-side, and the automatic retry runs the full generation again. Finance later notes the usage for the window is roughly double what the successful-response count implies. What is the correct understanding?",
o:["A. Abandoned requests are not billed, so the discrepancy must come from a separate workload","B. A client-side timeout does not cancel server-side work: the first generation likely completed and was billed, so a retry pays twice — the fix is a timeout above the p99 of the work actually issued, plus streaming for genuinely long calls","C. The SDK deduplicates identical retries server-side, so only one generation should have been billed and this is a metering fault to escalate","D. Setting `max_tokens` lower would have made the first attempt cheaper and eliminated the double billing"],a:1,
e:"A timeout is **ambiguous by construction** — the client learns only that *it* stopped waiting, never whether the server finished. Work already in flight generally continues and is metered, so an aggressive timeout plus automatic retry silently doubles cost on every slow call while the success count records one result. Three fixes, in order: set the timeout **above the p99 latency of the work you actually issue** (per call path, not one global value); **stream** long generations so the connection stays productive and progress is observable; and, where a retry touches side effects, make the innermost mutation **idempotent** so ambiguity cannot cause duplicate effects as well as duplicate cost.",
w:"A — abandoning a connection does not retract work already performed; the tokens were generated. C — there is no cross-request deduplication of identical calls, and the metering is consistent with two generations having run. D — a lower ceiling reduces the cost of each attempt but leaves the double-attempt pattern, and does not affect output-token rate limits either."},

{id:1225,d:"D1",s:"Error Handling & Retries",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"On Claude Platform on AWS, a failing call needs to be traced both in the organisation's own AWS audit tooling and in an Anthropic support ticket. Which identifiers apply?",
o:["A. Two: `x-amzn-requestid`, the primary id indexed in CloudTrail, and `request-id`, the Anthropic id to quote to support","B. The AWS SigV4 signature, which serves as the per-call identifier on that platform","C. A single `request-id`, which AWS mirrors into its audit trail automatically","D. Only `x-amzn-requestid`, since Anthropic identifiers are not issued on partner platforms"],a:0,
e:"On that platform responses carry **two** request identifiers, and they are not interchangeable. **`x-amzn-requestid`** is the primary id and the one **indexed in CloudTrail**, so it is what you use for AWS-side audit and lookup. **`request-id`** is the secondary, Anthropic-issued id — the value to quote in an **Anthropic support ticket**. Log both on every call. The SDKs' raw-response accessors read either header, and the Anthropic id is also available through the usual `_request_id`-style property. Same principle as always: identifiers are cheap to log and impossible to reconstruct after the fact.",
w:"C — two distinct ids exist and neither system indexes the other's, which is exactly why both must be logged. B — a request signature is an authentication artefact, not a traceable per-call identifier. D — the Anthropic `request-id` is present on that platform as the secondary identifier and is the one support needs."},

{id:1226,d:"D1",s:"Error Handling & Retries",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A circuit breaker trips after sustained 529s. The team must decide how it closes again. Which behaviour is correct?",
o:["A. Close automatically after a fixed cool-down, since the outage duration is predictable from historical incidents","B. Stay open until an operator manually resets it, so no traffic is sent to a dependency of unknown health","C. Close as soon as any queued request succeeds, since one success proves recovery","D. After the open interval, enter half-open and allow a small number of trial requests; close on success, and re-open immediately on failure"],a:3,
e:"The **half-open** state is what makes a breaker self-healing without being reckless. While **open**, calls fail fast and the dependency gets zero load. After the open interval the breaker admits a **small, bounded number of probe requests**: if they succeed the breaker **closes** and full traffic resumes; if any fails it **re-opens** and the interval restarts (often with backoff). The point is to test recovery with a trickle rather than a flood — closing straight to full traffic re-creates the thundering herd that the breaker was protecting against. Pair with jittered backoff underneath and a bounded retry budget above.",
w:"A — closing blind to full traffic after a timer sends a synchronised flood at a dependency whose health is unverified. B — a manual reset means every incident needs a human even after the platform has recovered, extending outages needlessly. C — a single success is weak evidence and, worse, it lets the breaker close to full traffic on one data point."},

{id:1227,d:"D1",s:"Error Handling & Retries",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A team adds idempotency keys to a side-effecting tool. Their helper generates a fresh UUID at the start of every attempt and passes it as the key. Duplicate side effects continue after timeouts. What is wrong?",
o:["A. The key must be stable per logical operation, not per attempt: a new key on each retry looks like a new operation to the receiver, so nothing is deduplicated","B. UUIDs are unsuitable as idempotency keys; the receiver requires a monotonically increasing integer","C. The key must be sent as an HTTP header rather than in the request body to be honoured","D. Idempotency keys only apply to the Claude API call, not to downstream tool invocations"],a:0,
e:"An idempotency key is a **statement of identity**: \"this is the same operation you may already have seen.\" Regenerating it per attempt asserts the opposite, so the receiver correctly treats each retry as new work. The key must be derived from the **logical operation** — invoice id, order id, or a UUID minted **once** when the operation is first created and then persisted and reused for every attempt. It must survive process restarts too, or a crash-then-retry duplicates. Transferable rule: **the key is generated where the intent originates, not where the retry happens**.",
w:"B — a UUID is a perfectly good key; the defect is when it is generated, not what format it takes. C — transport placement is a receiver-specific convention and is not what breaks deduplication here. D — the whole point is that side effects live in downstream tools; that is exactly where idempotency must be enforced, since the model call itself has no side effects."},

{id:1228,d:"D1",s:"Error Handling & Retries",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"What does the `retry-after` header on a 429 response tell a client, and what happens if the client retries sooner?",
o:["A. It is an advisory average wait; retrying sooner has a proportionally lower chance of success","B. It is the number of seconds to wait before retrying, and earlier retries will fail","C. It is the RFC 3339 timestamp at which the limit will be fully replenished","D. It is the number of retries remaining within the current rate-limit window"],a:1,
e:"`retry-after` is **a number of seconds to wait, and retrying earlier will fail** — it is server-authoritative, reflecting your actual bucket state, which no client-side heuristic can infer. So treat it as a **floor**: `wait = max(retry_after, jittered_backoff)`, never a minimum you feel free to undercut. Two companion facts: it is **not always present** — the tier spend-cap 429 deliberately omits it, because no retry will succeed until access resumes — so a missing header must fall back to jittered backoff, never to zero. And do not confuse it with the `anthropic-ratelimit-*-reset` headers, which are **absolute RFC 3339 timestamps**, not durations.",
w:"A — the docs are unambiguous that earlier retries fail; treating it as probabilistic invites wasted attempts and extra load. C — that is the format and meaning of the `-reset` headers; `retry-after` is a relative number of seconds. D — no header reports a remaining retry count; remaining capacity is reported by the `anthropic-ratelimit-*-remaining` headers."},

{id:1229,d:"D1",s:"Error Handling & Retries",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"An organisation runs three workloads on one model: customer-facing chat, an internal analytics assistant, and a nightly bulk enrichment job. Under 429 pressure all three back off equally, so customer chat degrades alongside the batch job. Which change best matches business priority?",
o:["A. Increase the retry ceiling for chat so its requests persist longer during contention","B. Have all three workloads share one retry policy, tuned to the slowest acceptable latency","C. Introduce priority classes at the shared limiter — admit customer traffic first and shed or defer lower-priority classes under pressure, rather than distributing scarcity uniformly","D. Split the workloads across three API keys so each backs off independently"],a:2,
e:"Rate limiting makes capacity scarce; **something must decide who gets it**. Equal backoff is a decision — to distribute scarcity uniformly — and it is almost never the business's preference. **Priority-aware admission** at the shared limiter encodes the real ranking: customer traffic is admitted first, the internal assistant is queued or slowed, and bulk enrichment is deferred entirely (ideally onto the **Batch API**, which has its own separate limits, or onto a **different model class**, since limits apply per model). Transferable principle: **under scarcity, shed load by value, not uniformly** — and decide it centrally, where total demand is visible.",
w:"A — a longer ceiling makes chat wait longer rather than jump the queue, and it adds load during contention. B — one shared policy is precisely what causes uniform degradation; it cannot express that chat matters more than an overnight job. D — rate limits are enforced at the organisation level, so extra keys draw from the same buckets and grant no isolation."},

{id:1230,d:"D1",s:"Error Handling & Retries",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A post-incident review examines the retry layer's telemetry. Of the retries fired over the last month, 0.4% eventually succeeded; the rest exhausted their budget. What does that ratio most strongly indicate, and what should change?",
o:["A. The backoff ceiling is too low, so retries give up before the dependency recovers; raise the ceiling and the attempt count","B. The retry layer is working as intended — a low success rate simply reflects a month with few transient faults","C. Jitter is being applied incorrectly, causing retries to land during the same congested windows","D. The retried failures are largely deterministic rather than transient, so the classifier is retrying errors that cannot succeed — tighten it to retry only connection faults, 429 (excluding the spend-cap variant), and 5xx"],a:3,
e:"**Retry-success ratio is the health metric for a retry policy.** Retries exist to convert transient faults into successes; a near-zero conversion rate says the inputs were not transient. Almost always the classifier is too broad — retrying 400s, 401s, 403s, 413s, spend-limit 400s, or the `retry-after`-less tier spend-cap 429 — so each retry is guaranteed-fail load that also inflates latency and hides the real defect behind a plausible-looking error rate. Tighten the classifier to genuinely retryable conditions, then **track the ratio per `error.type`** so the next regression is visible immediately.",
w:"A — raising the ceiling multiplies attempts against failures that cannot succeed, worsening both cost and time-to-detection. B — a low ratio is not neutral: it means retry load is being spent on work with no prospect of success, which is exactly what the metric exists to catch. C — jitter affects the timing distribution of retries, not whether the underlying error class is capable of succeeding on a later attempt."}
);
